#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build once and run the touched tests within a per-package wall-clock/CPU budget.

Usage: scripts/scoped-test [--base origin/rolling] [--head HEAD] [--dry-run]
       scripts/scoped-test --filter PACKAGE=FILTER [--filter PACKAGE=FILTER ...]
Explicit filters replace diff-derived filters, including on an unchanged base.
Use --repo PATH to reproduce in an existing base worktree; execution always
requires --head to match that worktree's HEAD. Filters use the lander's plain
library names, shard:SHARD:test(ATOM), bin:TARGET and test:TARGET forms. Named
targets also accept :test(ATOM), e.g. --filter 'rsid=bin:tool:test(=tests::one)'.
An ATOM is a substring, =exact::name, or /regex/.
--total-max-sec caps the whole run: each package's budget is the lesser of
--runtime-max-sec and the time left, and a package with none left is not run
(`scoped_test_timeout: total budget exhausted`, exit 1). The durable recipes
use it so their sequential package budgets fit the job's own cap (#1584).
Daemon scoped_test jobs also pass --job-max-sec: slot/lock waits spend that
wall budget and admission leaves a 120s runtime reserve (#2025). One admission
is held across the selected packages (#2216); each keeps its own bounded unit.
RSI_SCOPED_TEST_FILTERS (newline-separated PACKAGE=FILTER, set by the daemon's
recipe `filters` param) acts as --filter when no --filter is given.
Use --reclaim-incremental to remove Cargo incremental caches after each successful
package when another selected package remains; compiled dependencies and test
binaries stay available.
Linux uses a foreground systemd service in the hub's shared build slice
`rsi-build.slice` (#2119): its CPUQuota is the operator's `hub_build_cpu_percent` of the host's
threads, so concurrent runs share one budget (RSI_BUILD_SLICE overrides the name, empty runs
outside it). Other platforms use a plain process timeout and print that no slice or CPU cap is
available. Logs and the derived plan live in /tmp/rsi-scoped-test-*.
Static guards run even for dry runs, declined plans, and explicit filters.
A package whose selected tests passed retains its passing verdict and count even when another
filter is empty. `unmatched_filters` names those filters separately and still makes the run red.
`baseline_reds` requires a named candidate failure observed again in a failing base run;
a failed build or harness alone never establishes one. `baseline_failure_records` separately
annotates active known-failure records by name (not a verified signature match), and
`baseline_reds_without_record` names successful lookups with no record. Durable jobs use the
shared classifier snapshot without agent credentials; a missing/stale snapshot or failed lookup
is `unavailable`, never evidence that no record exists. Lookup time is bounded to ten seconds.
Execution order is fixed (#2647): the selected Python tests (stdlib `python3 -m unittest`
for the repo suites) run first, fail-fast, and Cargo starts only after they all pass. The run prints the order
(`execution order: ...`) and records it as the receipt's `phase_order`. `--dry-run` selects the Python
tests with the same code and prints each `python test:` command and the Cargo plan, fills `python` with
`planned` entries (a missing runner is the typed `runner_unavailable` entry, not a pass), and executes no
test and no Cargo, so a worker can run exactly those Python commands in the foreground before it submits.
The `desktop` lane (hub #3001, `scripts/desktop-test-gate.py`) fails closed (#355): a candidate whose diff touches
the script (a delete or rename) or a lane input while the script is missing is refused, never an empty lane.
It is selected by the same rule the merge queue
uses: a diff that touches `desktop/`, `docs/keybindings.md`, `crates/rsi/src` or a path dependency of
`desktop/src-tauri/Cargo.toml` (`trigger_inputs` in the gate script) runs `npm ci` + `npm test` and `cargo test` in `desktop/src-tauri` on a
private copy of the tree (npm cache and Cargo target under the job's TMPDIR/target, never the committed
tree); a diff elsewhere selects nothing. It runs after the Python tests and before Cargo. Desktop failures
keep the run red while Cargo and the warning gate still run; timeouts stop immediately. Its
receipt entries are the `desktop` list (`status` passed/failed/runner_unavailable/no_tests_executed/planned,
`npm_tests`, `cargo_tests`, `executed_tests`). A missing node, npm, cargo or (Linux) webkit2gtk-4.1 is the
typed `runner_unavailable` with zero tests, never green; `--dry-run` previews the lane and runs nothing.
Planning starts no cargo (#2564): derivation is `check-touched-shards --filters-only` and a
dry run only prints the build it would run. The receipt's `compile` object records
`planning` (source, `cargo_invoked: false`), `execution` (`pending`, `skipped_dry_run`,
`executed`, `none_scheduled`) and each package's build as `executed`/`failed`/`incomplete`.
By default the selected tests run with the merge-queue lander's tmpfs parity
(#1182, #1910): a private TMPDIR under /dev/shm (override the parent with
CHECK_TOUCHED_SHARDS_TMPDIR); the build still uses the host TMPDIR and the same
Cargo target. This is one test pass, not a second verification job.
The selected Python fixture tests run under the same kind of private TMPDIR and
print `TMPFS_PARITY python tests=N tmpfs=<dir>` (also `python_tmpfs` in the
receipt), so a script-only change is never silently unchecked (#1799).
Where no tmpfs exists (non-Linux, no usable /dev/shm, or one that leaves this
user under RSI_SCOPED_TEST_TMPFS_MIN_GB, default 12, counting the user's block
quota and not only df's free space, #2177) the default prints
`TMPFS_PARITY unavailable: ...` and runs the same selected tests with the host
TMPDIR. The receipt records scratch_mode and completed tests. Explicit --tmpfs
remains a parity-only request: it reports unavailable and skips when no tmpfs
exists, for compatibility with existing callers.
Tests run with cargo-nextest when it is installed (receipt `runner: "nextest"`): one
`cargo nextest run -E 'test(=a) | ...'` per batch of exact names at N test threads, hangs named
through the repo profile's slow-timeout/terminate-after (`.config/nextest.toml`). Without it
(laptop, macOS) they run in N concurrent single-threaded libtest processes per package, names
interleaved across the shards, each with its own start/end tracking so a timeout names every
running test (#2046, `runner: "libtest"`). `--runner` or RSI_SCOPED_TEST_RUNNER forces one.
N is load-adaptive (#2084): clamp((host threads - 1-minute load) / 2, 2, 12), never above the
job's `--cpu-quota` in cores; the worker unit's CPUQuota is N*100%. `--test-shards` overrides N.
The receipt records runner, test_threads, cpu_quota, host_threads, load1 and adaptive_threads.
A plan of more than --filter-budget (default 12) distinct filters is never broadened to
whole packages (#2084 replaces #2047's `PKG=*`): it runs as named, most specific filters first,
inside a test-time box (--test-time-box-sec, default 600; 0 = no box). Packages and tiers not
started when the box is spent are skipped, not failed: the receipt says `plan:
"targeted_subset"` with selected_filters/run_filters, selected_tests/run_tests, box_expired,
filters_partial and filters_not_run, and the merge-queue gate covers the rest. Filter coverage derives
from the executed selected test identities (#216): a filter whose every selected test ran is `run`
even when the box expired afterwards, a part-run one is `filters_partial` with its selected/executed
counts, a filter with no executed test is `filters_not_run`; a failed test that ran counts as
executed and never changes the verdict; `unlisted_packages` names packages that never listed.
The tests of a module whose own source changed run right after the added/changed ones and before
any unrelated observer, smallest module first (#2182, check-touched-shards `changed_modules`); a
module that selects more than CHANGED_MODULE_TEST_LIMIT tests is a broad filter and keeps its
specificity tier. The receipt names `changed_modules` and, in a targeted subset,
`changed_module_tests_selected`/`changed_module_tests_run`. Exit is green when the run subset
passes. A BROAD observer fallback from check-touched-shards (#2105: a trace over 150 functions
or 24 tests keeps the module filter) is never refused: it runs boxed the same way and the
receipt carries `uncertain: "broad_observers"` with `broad_filter_count`, `broad_tests` and
`broad_filters` (filter, tests, reason); the merge-queue gate and the full-library QA cover the
rest. The same expression under several shard labels (the label only routes it, #1698) is one
selection: `broad_filter_count` and `broad_tests` count each expression once, and
`broad_filter_entries` keeps the labelled count. A trace that stops at a high-fan-in hub (a type more than 150 functions mention) or at the
function, test or identity bound still names the tests it proved observe the change
(`direct_observers`, `broad_filters[].direct_tests`/`hubs`/`trace_stopped`): they run nearest first
right after the added/changed and changed-module tests and ahead of every unranked far test, whatever
specificity tier the far test is in (an exact filter's unrelated test included), in every binary of the
package, and the packages holding them run first after the changed-module packages (#2588). Where an
ambiguity or an untraceable diff keeps the broad filter, every selected test stays selected: far tests are
ordered last, never dropped. The hint only orders tests the plan already selects, so filter counts and
executed identities are the plan's own (`direct_observer_tests_selected`/`_run`). The tiers of all of a
package's binaries run in one rank order, so the box cannot be spent in an earlier binary first.
A trace that stopped at a CAP (a hub, the function bound or the test bound, every identity placed; the
broad entry's `worker_bounded`) is never widened for the worker (#2592). Its module filter stays in
`suggested_filters`, which is the merge-queue gate's selection, but the worker plan keeps only that module's
changed-module tests (up to CHANGED_MODULE_TEST_LIMIT per module) and the proven direct observers, nearest
first inside the box. Every direct observer is selected in its own right (`direct_only`, built with its
package), also one that sits outside every plan filter; these are not filters of the plan and have no coverage
entry. The named filters the module filter had absorbed (`merged_filters`) return to the plan. Everything else the filter matches is DEFERRED: never selected, run or counted passed. The receipt
carries `worker_policy: "bounded_deferred"`, `worker_broad_filters` (broad worker filters left: 0 when every
broad entry was a cap), `filters_bounded` (per filter kept/executed/deferred, apart from run/partial/not_run)
and `deferred`: the filters and why they stopped, `scope_tests_at_most` from the derivation (before the kept
tests are subtracted; the dry run's number), `tests` (exact deferred test identities counted from the package
listings once a run lists them; `tests_basis` says if a package never listed), `routes`
(`merge_queue_gate`, `pinned_full_qa`), `candidate` (the pinned SHA) and `status: "pending"`: the receipt
never says that gate or QA ran, only the queue entry and the `qa_green` field of `AgentListRollingQueue` show that.
A non-zero derivation exit names its cause in `derive_failure` (for example a candidate
that no longer merges cleanly with the base). An ordinary plan reports `plan: "precise"`. `--filter-budget 0` keeps the precise plan
and runs everything it names.

Compiler warnings (#1897): when the repository checks in `tools/warning-baseline.json`, the build this run already
supervises is also the evidence for that file. The compiler diagnostics of the JSON stream are attributed to their
Cargo package and counted once per file, lint code and message (a line number only tells two reports of one warning
apart, so the lib and lib-test units do not double a count). A baselined package built in the run may not have a
warning key above its baseline count (`warning_gate_failed: new_warnings`), the candidate's baseline may not be above
the merge base's or drop a package it has (`baseline_raised`), and diagnostics that cannot be trusted (flags that allow
warnings, a diagnostic outside the JSON stream, a warning with no package) fail the run as `unavailable` rather than
count as clean. A package the run did not build is `not_measured`, never clean. The receipt's `warnings` object
carries the verdict. `--warning-baseline-from LOG` prints the `packages` document a finished job log measured, to
seed or lower the baseline.

Trial merge (#348): at the end of a run that resolved its head, a fetch-free
`git merge-tree --write-tree origin/rolling <head>` is recorded as the receipt's
`merges_cleanly_onto_rolling` (true, false, or null with `merge_check_reason`) and, when false,
`conflict_paths` (first 32; `conflict_paths_total` is the full count). It is informational: it
never changes the exit code or verdict. A worker whose receipt says false merges origin/rolling
(no rebase), resolves, pushes and reruns one scoped_test.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import importlib.util
import json
import math
import platform
import os
from pathlib import Path
import queue
import re
import runpy
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
import uuid

SCRIPT = Path(__file__).resolve()
_python_spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("python_test_gate", SCRIPT.with_name("python-test-gate.py"))
PYTHON_GATE = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_python_spec)
_python_spec.loader.exec_module(PYTHON_GATE)
_desktop_spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("desktop_test_gate", SCRIPT.with_name("desktop-test-gate.py"))
DESKTOP_GATE = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_desktop_spec)
_desktop_spec.loader.exec_module(DESKTOP_GATE)


def command_text(command):
    return shlex.join(map(str, command))


def signal_group(process, sig):
    """Signal the process group we created; a group with nobody left to signal is already stopped.

    macOS answers killpg(2) with EPERM, not ESRCH, when the group's only members are
    zombies or exiting (the leader exited but is not reaped yet). That is the same
    "already gone" outcome as ProcessLookupError, so it must not replace the
    TimeoutExpired that brought us here. Fall back to the leader alone; if even that
    is refused for a live process, name the step and the errno.
    """
    try:
        os.killpg(process.pid, sig)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        pass
    except PermissionError as error:
        if process.poll() is not None:
            return  # the leader is reaped and no member can be signalled
        try:
            process.send_signal(sig)
        except ProcessLookupError:
            pass
        except PermissionError:
            raise PermissionError(error.errno,
                                  f"cannot signal process group {process.pid} with {sig.name} "
                                  f"({error.strerror})") from error


def stop_process(process, own_tree=True):
    """Stop only the process tree we created (never search for other processes)."""
    if os.name == "nt":
        subprocess.run(["taskkill", "/PID", str(process.pid), "/T", "/F"], check=False)
    elif own_tree:
        signal_group(process, signal.SIGTERM)
        try:
            process.wait(timeout=2)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            pass
        signal_group(process, signal.SIGKILL)
    else:
        process.terminate()
    process.wait()


class Budget:
    """A run budget whose clock starts when the governor grants the build slot (#1756).

    Time queued behind other cargo work never counts: `scripts/cargo-slot` prints
    `rsi-phase <epoch> slot-granted: ...` when the slot is granted, and `grant()`
    starts the clock then. A budget with no slot to wait for starts at once.
    `wait_cap` bounds the queue; an optional job deadline includes queue time.
    `waited` is the seconds spent queued.
    """

    GRANT = re.compile(r"rsi-phase \d+ slot-granted:")

    def __init__(self, seconds, queued=False, wait_cap=600, grace=0, deadline=None):
        self.seconds, self.wait_cap, self.grace = seconds, wait_cap, grace
        self.deadline = deadline
        self.created = time.monotonic()
        self.started = None if queued else self.created

    def observe(self, line):
        if self.started is None and self.GRANT.match(line):
            self.started = time.monotonic()

    @property
    def waited(self):
        return (self.started if self.started is not None else time.monotonic()) - self.created

    def remaining(self):
        """Seconds left; the wait cap while still queued."""
        if self.started is None:
            left = self.created + self.wait_cap - time.monotonic()
        else:
            left = self.started + self.seconds + self.grace - time.monotonic()
        if self.deadline is not None:
            left = min(left, self.deadline - time.monotonic())
        return max(0, left)

    def ran_out(self):
        """The run (not the queue) used its whole budget."""
        return self.started is not None and time.monotonic() - self.started >= self.seconds


def stream(command, emit, timeout=None, stop=None, own_tree=True, cwd=None, env=None, budget=None):
    """Tee output live; a timeout covers silent commands as well as noisy ones.

    `budget` (a Budget) replaces `timeout` and re-reads the deadline as output arrives.
    """
    process = subprocess.Popen(command, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                               text=True, errors="replace", start_new_session=own_tree and os.name != "nt", cwd=cwd, env=env)
    lines = queue.Queue()

    def read():
        try:
            for line in process.stdout:
                lines.put(line)
        finally:
            lines.put(None)
            process.stdout.close()

    reader = threading.Thread(target=read, daemon=True)
    reader.start()
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout if timeout is not None else None

    def left():
        if budget is not None:
            return budget.remaining()
        return None if deadline is None else max(0, deadline - time.monotonic())

    try:
        while True:
            remaining = left()
            try:
                line = lines.get(timeout=remaining)
            except queue.Empty:
                raise subprocess.TimeoutExpired(command, timeout)
            if line is None:
                return process.wait(timeout=left())
            if budget is not None:
                budget.observe(line)
            emit(line)
    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, KeyboardInterrupt):
        try:
            if stop:
                stop()
        finally:
            stop_process(process, own_tree)
        raise


def user_free_bytes(path):
    """Bytes this user can still write under `path`: the filesystem's free space capped by the
    user's block quota there (#2177, mirrors `user_free_gb` in scripts/cargo-slot, #938).
    systemd gives each user a quota of 80% of the /tmp and /dev/shm tmpfs mounts and statvfs
    reports the whole filesystem, so a statvfs-sized scratch dir fails with "Disk quota exceeded".
    quotactl_fd(2) is Linux 5.14+ (tmpfs quotas 6.6+); no quota, or no way to read one, leaves
    the free space uncapped. An unreadable `path` has 0 bytes."""
    try:
        stats = os.statvfs(path)
    except OSError:
        return 0
    free = stats.f_bavail * stats.f_frsize
    # quotactl_fd is syscall 443 on every Linux ABI except the offset mips/alpha ones.
    if sys.platform.startswith("linux") and not platform.machine().startswith(("mips", "alpha")):
        try:
            import ctypes
            import struct
            fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY)
            try:
                dqblk = ctypes.create_string_buffer(72)  # struct if_dqblk
                command = (0x800007 << 8) | 0  # QCMD(Q_GETQUOTA, USRQUOTA)
                libc = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True)
                if libc.syscall(443, fd, ctypes.c_uint(command), ctypes.c_uint(os.geteuid()), dqblk) == 0:
                    hard_kib, _soft, used = struct.unpack_from("=QQQ", dqblk.raw)
                    valid = struct.unpack_from("=I", dqblk.raw, 64)[0]
                    if hard_kib and (valid & 5) == 5:  # QIF_BLIMITS | QIF_SPACE
                        free = min(free, max(0, hard_kib * 1024 - used))
            finally:
                os.close(fd)
        except (OSError, AttributeError, ImportError):
            pass
    return free


def tmpfs_min_free_gb():
    """The floor, in GiB, the user's room under the tmpfs parent must reach (the lander's own
    --tmpfs-min-free-gb default, 12); RSI_SCOPED_TEST_TMPFS_MIN_GB overrides it."""
    value = os.environ.get("RSI_SCOPED_TEST_TMPFS_MIN_GB", "")
    return int(value) if value.isdigit() else 12


def tmpfs_parent():
    """The tmpfs directory the lander gates on, or (None, reason) when unavailable.

    A parent that leaves this user less than the floor (free space or block quota,
    whichever is smaller) is unavailable too: the run uses the host TMPDIR instead of
    failing with "Disk quota exceeded" (#2177, #565)."""
    if not sys.platform.startswith("linux"):
        return None, "no tmpfs on " + sys.platform
    parent = os.environ.get("CHECK_TOUCHED_SHARDS_TMPDIR", "/dev/shm")
    if not (os.path.isdir(parent) and os.access(parent, os.W_OK | os.X_OK)):
        return None, f"{parent} is not a writable directory"
    floor_gb = tmpfs_min_free_gb()
    room_gb = user_free_bytes(parent) // (1 << 30)
    if room_gb < floor_gb:
        return None, f"{parent} leaves this user {room_gb} GiB (quota included), below {floor_gb} GiB"
    return parent, ""


def selection_plan(filters, shard_packages, priority_filters=()):
    plan = {}
    for value in dict.fromkeys([*filters, *priority_filters]):
        package, sep, expression = value.partition("=")
        if not sep or not re.fullmatch(r"[\w-]+", package) or not expression:
            raise ValueError(f"refusing unscoped filter: {value}")
        packages, kind, target, substring = [package], "lib", "", expression
        exact = regex = atom = None
        if expression.startswith("shard:"):
            match = re.fullmatch(r"shard:([\w-]+):test\((.+)\)", expression)
            if not match or package not in ("rsid", "rsid-store"):
                raise ValueError(f"unsupported shard filter: {value}")
            shard, atom = match.groups()
            packages = shard_packages.get(shard)
            if not packages:
                raise ValueError(f"unknown shard: {shard}")
        elif expression.startswith(("bin:", "test:")):
            match = re.fullmatch(r"(bin|test):([\w-]+)(?::test\((.+)\))?", expression)
            if not match:
                raise ValueError(f"invalid test target: {value}")
            kind, target, atom = match.groups()
            substring = ""
        elif not re.fullmatch(r"[\w:]+", expression):
            raise ValueError(f"unsupported filter: {value}")
        if atom is not None:
            # `test(name)` is a substring; `test(=name)` one exact test; `test(/regex/)` a
            # regular expression (check-touched-shards emits it for exact module paths, #1440).
            if re.fullmatch(r"[\w:]+", atom):
                substring = atom
            elif atom.startswith("=") and re.fullmatch(r"[\w:]+", atom[1:]):
                exact = atom[1:]
            elif re.fullmatch(r"/[\w:^$|()?.*+\[\]\\-]+/", atom):
                regex = atom[1:-1]
                re.compile(regex)
            else:
                raise ValueError(f"unsupported test atom: {value}")
        for owner in packages:
            plan.setdefault(owner, []).append({"filter": value, "kind": kind,
                                               "target": target, "substring": substring,
                                               "exact": exact, "regex": regex,
                                               "priority": value in priority_filters})
    return plan


# A plan with more filters than this is a targeted subset (#2084): it runs as named, most
# specific first, inside a test-time box, and the merge-queue gate covers the rest. Matches
# the derivation's GATE_FILTER_LIMIT; `--filter-budget 0` keeps the precise plan unboxed.
FILTER_BUDGET = 12
TEST_TIME_BOX_SEC = 600
THREADS_MIN, THREADS_MAX = 2, 12


def plan_filter_count(plan):
    return len({s["filter"] for selections in plan.values() for s in selections if not s.get("direct_only")})


def package_order(plan, changed_packages=(), direct_packages=()):
    """Added/changed tests first, then packages that own a changed module (#2182), then packages
    holding a proven direct observer of a bounded broad trace (#2588), then the most specific:
    fewest broad (non-exact, non-regex) filters, then fewest filters."""
    def key(item):
        package, selections = item
        broad = sum(1 for s in selections if not (s.get("exact") or s.get("regex")))
        return (not any(s.get("priority") for s in selections), package not in changed_packages,
                package not in direct_packages, broad, len(selections), package)
    return sorted(plan.items(), key=key)


def host_capacity():
    """(logical threads, 1-minute load or None), read at run time."""
    try:
        load = os.getloadavg()[0]
    except (AttributeError, OSError):
        load = None
    return os.cpu_count() or 2, load


def adaptive_threads(host_threads, load1):
    """clamp((host threads - 1-minute load) / 2, 2, 12): a busy host gets fewer test threads."""
    free = host_threads - (load1 or 0.0)
    return int(max(THREADS_MIN, min(THREADS_MAX, free / 2)))


def nextest_required(repo):
    """The minimum cargo-nextest version the repo's config demands, as a tuple (or ())."""
    try:
        text = (Path(repo) / ".config" / "nextest.toml").read_text()
    except OSError:
        return ()
    found = re.search(r'nextest-version\s*=\s*\{[^}]*required\s*=\s*"([\d.]+)"', text)
    return tuple(int(part) for part in found[1].split(".")) if found else ()


def detect_runner(repo, requested="auto"):
    """(runner, nextest version or None): nextest when installed and new enough, else libtest."""
    requested = os.environ.get("RSI_SCOPED_TEST_RUNNER") or requested
    if requested == "libtest":
        return "libtest", None
    binary = shutil.which("cargo-nextest")
    version = None
    if binary:
        try:
            done = subprocess.run([binary, "nextest", "--version"], text=True, capture_output=True, timeout=30)
            found = re.match(r"cargo-nextest (\d+\.\d+\.\d+)", done.stdout) if done.returncode == 0 else None
            version = found[1] if found else None
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            version = None
    if version and tuple(int(p) for p in version.split(".")) >= nextest_required(repo):
        return "nextest", version
    if requested == "nextest":
        raise ValueError("--runner nextest: cargo-nextest is missing or older than .config/nextest.toml requires")
    return "libtest", None


# The integration target whose tests return Ok at once unless RSI_E2E=1 and that spawn a
# prebuilt rsid; selecting it must set the flag and build rsid, or UI drift passes (#1596).
E2E_TARGET = "e2e_tui"


def selects_e2e(selections):
    return any(s["kind"] == "test" and s["target"] == E2E_TARGET for s in selections)


E2E_PREBUILD_LIST = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "e2e-prebuild-bins.txt"


def e2e_prebuild_bins(path=E2E_PREBUILD_LIST):
    """(package, binary) pairs from the single shared definition (#1651)."""
    pairs = []
    for line in path.read_text().splitlines():
        line = line.split("#", 1)[0].strip()
        if line:
            package, binary = line.split()
            pairs.append((package, binary))
    return pairs


def e2e_prebuild_command(cargo):
    # The list is shared with rsi-rolling-land so the two cannot drift (#1637).
    pairs = e2e_prebuild_bins()
    args = ["build"]
    for package in dict.fromkeys(package for package, _ in pairs):
        args += ["-p", package]
    for _, binary in pairs:
        args += ["--bin", binary]
    return [*cargo, *args]


def build_command(package, selections, cargo):
    # execute_package removes this variable itself; Windows has no env -u.
    if os.name == "nt" and cargo[:3] == ["env", "-u", "RSI_PROCESS_OWNERSHIP_NAMESPACE"]:
        cargo = cargo[3:]
    targets = []
    if any(s["kind"] == "lib" for s in selections):
        targets.append("--lib")
    for kind, target in sorted({(s["kind"], s["target"]) for s in selections if s["kind"] != "lib"}):
        targets.extend(["--" + kind, target])
    return [*cargo, "test", "-p", package, *targets, "--no-run", "--message-format=json"]


def execute_package(plan):
    """Inside the budget: one build, list binaries, run each selected test once."""
    os.chdir(plan["repo"])
    os.environ.pop("RSI_PROCESS_OWNERSHIP_NAMESPACE", None)
    artifacts = {}
    diagnosed = []
    warnings = WarningCollector(plan["repo"])

    def diagnose(text):
        """The first actionable compiler line, once, bounded (#1913)."""
        text = " ".join(str(text).split())[:400]
        if text and not diagnosed:
            diagnosed.append(text)
            print("SCOPED_DIAG " + json.dumps(text), flush=True)

    def build_line(line):
        try:
            message = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            print(line, end="", flush=True)
            warnings.text(line)
            if line.startswith(("error", "Error")):
                diagnose(line)
            return
        if message.get("reason") == "compiler-artifact":
            warnings.artifact(message)
        elif message.get("reason") == "compiler-message":
            warnings.diagnostic(message)
        if message.get("reason") == "compiler-artifact" and message.get("executable") and message.get("profile", {}).get("test"):
            artifacts[message["executable"]] = (
                message["target"], str(Path(message.get("manifest_path", Path(plan["repo"]) / "Cargo.toml")).parent))
        elif message.get("reason") == "compiler-message":
            detail = message["message"]
            print(detail.get("rendered", line), end="", flush=True)
            if detail.get("level") == "error":
                spans = [span for span in detail.get("spans") or [] if span.get("is_primary")]
                where = f" ({spans[0]['file_name']}:{spans[0]['line_start']})" if spans else ""
                diagnose(str(detail.get("message", "")) + where)
        elif message.get("reason") != "compiler-artifact":
            print(line, end="", flush=True)

    result = stream(plan["build"], build_line, own_tree=False)
    if result:
        print("SCOPED_PHASE build_failed", flush=True)
        return result
    print("SCOPED_WARNINGS " + json.dumps(warnings.payload(), separators=(",", ":")), flush=True)
    if plan.get("e2e"):
        os.environ["RSI_E2E"] = "1"
        print("scoped-test: e2e_tui selected; RSI_E2E=1 and building rsid for the harness", flush=True)
        result = stream(plan["e2e"]["prebuild"], lambda line: print(line, end="", flush=True), own_tree=False)
        if result:
            print("SCOPED_PHASE build_failed", flush=True)
            return result
    if not artifacts:
        print("scoped-test: build produced no test binaries", flush=True)
        print("SCOPED_PHASE build_failed", flush=True)
        return 1
    test_env, scratch = None, None
    if plan.get("tmpfs"):
        scratch = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="rsi-scoped-test-tmpfs-", dir=plan["tmpfs"])
        test_env = {**os.environ, "TMPDIR": scratch, "TMP": scratch, "TEMP": scratch}
        print(f"TMPFS_PARITY tmpfs={scratch}", flush=True)
    try:
        return run_selected(plan, artifacts, test_env, plan.get("exact_tests"))
    finally:
        if scratch:
            shutil.rmtree(scratch, ignore_errors=True)


BATCH_MAX_TESTS = 256
BATCH_MAX_BYTES = 64 * 1024


# A test selected by a filter that names few tests runs before the tests only a broad
# filter selects, so a budget that stops the run leaves the targeted evidence (#1732).
SPECIFICITY_TIERS = (24, 60)


def ranked_tiers(selected, selection_sizes, priority_tests=(), changed_ranks=None, direct_ranks=None):
    """[(rank key, sorted names)] of `selected`, first to run first.

    The key is (not added/changed, not in a changed module, that module's size, direct distance,
    specificity tier). Added/changed tests run first; then the tests of a changed module, smallest
    module first (`changed_ranks`, #2182); then every proven direct observer of a bounded broad
    trace, nearest first (`direct_ranks`, #2588), whatever filter selects it, so an unrelated exact
    observer can never spend a box before one; the unranked far tests follow by specificity: the
    size of the narrowest filter selecting each, tightest first (#1732). Each tier is sorted,
    because libtest runs a batch in name order. The keys compare across binaries."""
    changed_ranks, direct_ranks = changed_ranks or {}, direct_ranks or {}
    tiers = {}
    for name in sorted(selected):
        size = selection_sizes[name]
        tier = (name not in priority_tests, name not in changed_ranks, changed_ranks.get(name, 0),
                direct_ranks.get(name, FAR_RANK), sum(size > limit for limit in SPECIFICITY_TIERS))
        tiers.setdefault(tier, []).append(name)
    return [(tier, tiers[tier]) for tier in sorted(tiers)]


def specificity_tiers(selected, selection_sizes, priority_tests=(), changed_ranks=None, direct_ranks=None):
    """The names of `ranked_tiers`, first to run first."""
    return [names for _, names in ranked_tiers(selected, selection_sizes, priority_tests, changed_ranks, direct_ranks)]


# A selected test no bounded trace proved to observe the change ranks after every one that did.
FAR_RANK = 1 << 30


def direct_observer_ranks(names, selections):
    """{test name: nearest distance} of the `names` of one binary that a direct-observer selection matches.

    check-touched-shards names, for a broad filter whose trace had to stop (a high-fan-in hub, the
    function or test bound, an unresolved identity), the tests it proved observe the change, with
    the number of caller hops to each. They only order tests the plan already selects; a name no
    plan filter selects is never added here (#2588)."""
    ranks = {}
    for selection in selections:
        if selection.get("regex"):
            members = [n for n in names if re.search(selection["regex"], n)]
        elif selection.get("exact"):
            members = [n for n in names if n == selection["exact"]]
        else:
            members = [n for n in names if selection["substring"] in n]
        for name in members:
            ranks[name] = min(ranks.get(name, selection["distance"]), selection["distance"])
    return ranks


# A changed module whose own tests number more than this is itself a broad filter: ranking it
# ahead of every narrower observer would let it spend the whole time box, so its tests keep
# their specificity tier. The 155-test `session::rotation` of #1186 is well inside it.
CHANGED_MODULE_TEST_LIMIT = 300


def changed_module_ranks(names, records, target, limit=CHANGED_MODULE_TEST_LIMIT):
    """{test name: size of the smallest changed module or target holding it} for the `names` of one binary (#2182).

    A record from check-touched-shards' `changed_modules` holds the tests named `<module>::..`
    other than those of a descendant source it `excluded`, or every test of the changed bin or
    integration-test target. Only a record holding at most `limit` of the selected names ranks:
    a module that selects thousands is the broad fallback, not a changed module's own tests."""
    ranks = {}
    for record in records:
        if "module" in record:
            if "lib" not in target["kind"]:
                continue
            prefix, excluded = record["module"] + "::", tuple(x + "::" for x in record.get("excluded", ()))
            members = {n for n in names if n.startswith(prefix) and not n.startswith(excluded)}
        elif record.get("target") == target["name"] and record.get("kind") in target["kind"]:
            members = set(names)
        else:
            continue
        if not members or len(members) > limit:
            continue
        for name in members:
            ranks[name] = min(ranks.get(name, len(members)), len(members))
    return ranks


def test_batches(names, max_tests=BATCH_MAX_TESTS, max_bytes=BATCH_MAX_BYTES):
    """Split sorted test names into few libtest invocations (#1756).

    One process per test costs a start-up each (about 2.5 s for the lifecycle
    tests), which timed a broad same-module selection out. Batches are bounded by
    count and by argv bytes so one invocation stays well under ARG_MAX.
    """
    batch, size = [], 0
    for name in names:
        if batch and (len(batch) >= max_tests or size + len(name) + 1 > max_bytes):
            yield batch
            batch, size = [], 0
        batch.append(name)
        size += len(name) + 1
    if batch:
        yield batch


class BatchProgress:
    """Turn libtest's single-threaded `test NAME ... ` start lines into SCOPED_START/END.

    With --test-threads=1 libtest prints `test NAME ... ` before it runs the test, so a
    start line is a reliable boundary even when the test's own output precedes `ok`.
    """

    START = re.compile(r"test (\S+) \.\.\. ")

    def __init__(self, names, emit):
        self.names, self.emit, self.active, self.seen = set(names), emit, None, set()
        # Completion is the set of selected names seen to finish (#2079), never a raw line
        # count: a nested libtest child (an #[ignore]d fixture re-run by its parent test)
        # echoes `test X ... ok` for a name the batch also lists, and X's own line follows.
        self.done = set()
        self.extra = set()
        self.failures = set()
        self.failure_summary = False

    @property
    def ran(self):
        return len(self.done)

    def missing(self):
        return sorted(self.names - self.done)

    def note_extra(self, name):
        """Log a libtest-style line for a name this batch did not select, once."""
        if name not in self.extra:
            self.extra.add(name)
            self.emit(f"scoped-test: ignoring nested libtest line for unselected test {name}\n")

    def finish_active(self):
        if self.active is not None:
            self.emit("SCOPED_END " + self.active + "\n")
            self.done.add(self.active)
            self.active = None

    def begin(self, name):
        self.active = name
        self.seen.add(name)
        self.emit("SCOPED_START " + name + "\n")

    def line(self, text):
        found = self.START.match(text)
        if found and found[1] in self.names:
            self.failure_summary = False
            if found[1] != self.active and found[1] in self.done:
                # Already finished: a repeat from a nested child or the real line after
                # the child's echo. It is not a new test start.
                pass
            elif found[1] != self.active:
                self.finish_active()
                self.begin(found[1])
            if text.rstrip().endswith("FAILED"):
                self.failures.add(found[1])
        elif found:
            # A nested libtest child (or other process) printed a test line for a name
            # this batch did not select. Ignore it for completeness, but a FAILED
            # verdict is still a failure.
            self.note_extra(found[1])
            if text.rstrip().endswith("FAILED"):
                self.failures.add(found[1])
        elif self.active is not None and text.strip() == "FAILED":
            # --nocapture puts panic/output between the start and verdict.
            # Keep the active test's verdict even if a later test aborts the
            # process before libtest can print its failure summary (#1931).
            self.failures.add(self.active)
        elif text.strip() == "failures:":
            self.failure_summary = True
        elif self.failure_summary and text.startswith("    ") and text.strip() in self.names:
            # --nocapture often separates `test NAME ...` from `FAILED` with
            # panic or tracing output. The final libtest summary names it.
            self.failures.add(text.strip())
        elif text.startswith("test result: "):
            self.failure_summary = False
            self.finish_active()
        self.emit(text)


PRINT_LOCK = threading.Lock()


def emit_line(line):
    """One whole line at a time, so concurrent shards never split each other's lines."""
    with PRINT_LOCK:
        print(line, end="", flush=True)


def run_batch(binary, names, directory, test_env):
    """Run `names` exactly once in one libtest process; returns (tests run, exit code)."""
    progress = BatchProgress(names, emit_line)
    # libtest runs a sorted selection in order, so the first name is live at once: a hang
    # before any output still names its test.
    progress.begin(names[0])
    result = stream([binary, *names, "--exact", "--nocapture", "--test-threads=1"], progress.line,
                    own_tree=False, cwd=directory, env=test_env)
    progress.finish_active()
    if result == 0:
        # A successful binary may itself print failure-looking diagnostics.
        progress.failures.clear()
        # A clean exit ran every listed name; account for any whose start line the
        # test's own output hid.
        for name in names:
            if name not in progress.seen:
                progress.line(f"test {name} ... ok\n")
                progress.finish_active()
    return progress.ran, result, sorted(progress.failures), set(progress.done)


class NextestProgress:
    """Turn cargo-nextest status lines into the SCOPED_START/END protocol.

    nextest prints no start line, so a test is announced when it is SLOW (the repo profile's
    slow-timeout period) or when its verdict arrives; a hang is therefore named by its SLOW
    line and a terminate-after TIMEOUT fails it. Same surface as BatchProgress.
    """

    STATUS = re.compile(r"^\s*([A-Z][A-Z-]+)\s+\[\s*>?\s*[\d.]+s\]\s+\([^)]*\)\s+(\S+)\s+(\S.*?)\s*$")
    NOT_FINAL = {"SLOW", "TERMINATING", "DELAY", "RETRY", "START", "SKIP", "CANCEL", "CANCELLING"}
    PASSED = {"PASS", "LEAK"}

    def __init__(self, names, emit):
        self.names, self.emit = set(names), emit
        self.started, self.done, self.failures, self.extra = set(), set(), set(), set()

    @property
    def ran(self):
        return len(self.done)

    def missing(self):
        return sorted(self.names - self.done)

    def start(self, name):
        if name not in self.started:
            self.started.add(name)
            self.emit("SCOPED_START " + name + "\n")

    def line(self, text):
        found = self.STATUS.match(text)
        if found:
            status, name = found[1], found[3]
            if name not in self.names:
                if name not in self.extra and status not in self.NOT_FINAL:
                    self.extra.add(name)
                    self.emit(f"scoped-test: ignoring nextest line for unselected test {name}\n")
            elif status == "SLOW":
                self.start(name)
            elif status not in self.NOT_FINAL and name not in self.done:
                self.start(name)
                if status not in self.PASSED:
                    self.failures.add(name)
                self.done.add(name)
                self.emit("SCOPED_END " + name + "\n")
        self.emit(text)


def nextest_filterset(names):
    return " | ".join(f"test(={name})" for name in names)


def nextest_target_args(target):
    kinds = target["kind"]
    return ["--lib"] if "lib" in kinds else ["--bin", target["name"]] if "bin" in kinds else ["--test", target["name"]]


def run_nextest_batch(nextest, package, target, names, directory, test_env, threads):
    """Run exact `names` in one cargo-nextest invocation; returns run_batch's tuple.

    `--ignore-default-filter`: the names are explicit and the slow tier (#2270) was already
    decided by `run_selected`, so the profile's default-filter must not drop a named test
    (which `result == 0` below would then report as a pass)."""
    progress = NextestProgress(names, emit_line)
    command = [*nextest, "run", "-p", package, *nextest_target_args(target), "--no-fail-fast",
               "--color", "never", "--status-level", "pass", "--final-status-level", "none",
               "--test-threads", str(max(1, threads)), "--ignore-default-filter", "-E", nextest_filterset(names)]
    result = stream(command, progress.line, own_tree=False, cwd=directory, env=test_env)
    if result == 0:
        # A clean exit ran every listed name; account for any whose verdict line was hidden.
        for name in names:
            if name not in progress.done:
                progress.line(f"        PASS [   0.000s] (---/---) {package} {name}\n")
    return progress.ran, result, sorted(progress.failures), set(progress.done)


def interleave(names, shards):
    """Deal sorted `names` round-robin into at most `shards` lists; each stays sorted."""
    shards = max(1, min(shards, len(names)))
    return [names[index::shards] for index in range(shards)]


NEXTEST_BATCH_MAX_TESTS = 500
NEXTEST_BATCH_MAX_BYTES = 96 * 1024
# Under a time box a batch is the granularity at which the box is checked, so batches shrink.
BOXED_LIBTEST_BATCH, BOXED_NEXTEST_BATCH = 32, 120
NEXTEST_SAFE_NAME = re.compile(r"[\w:]+")


def run_tier(binary, tier, directory, test_env, target, shards, runner=None, deadline=None, ran_names=None):
    """Run one specificity tier; returns (tests run, whether any batch failed, box expired).

    libtest: `shards` concurrent single-threaded processes, each a thread running its own
    batches one process at a time, so every process keeps a single live test and its own
    BatchProgress. nextest (`runner` = (argv prefix, package, threads)): sequential batches,
    each one nextest run at `threads` test threads; a name nextest's filterset cannot carry
    falls back to libtest. A batch is not started once `deadline` (monotonic) has passed."""
    lock, total, failed, expired = threading.Lock(), [0], [False], [False]

    def settle(batch, outcome):
        ran, result, batch_failures, *rest = outcome
        with lock:
            total[0] += ran
            if ran_names is not None and rest:
                ran_names.update(rest[0])
            # Print as each batch settles so a later batch or package that times
            # out cannot lose them (#1895).
            for name in batch_failures:
                emit_line(f"SCOPED_FAILURE {target['kind'][0]}:{target['name']} {name}\n")
            failed[0] |= result != 0 or ran < len(batch)

    def out_of_time():
        if deadline is not None and time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            expired[0] = True
        return expired[0]

    def shard(names):
        if runner is None:
            for batch in (test_batches(names) if deadline is None else test_batches(names, BOXED_LIBTEST_BATCH)):
                if out_of_time():
                    return
                settle(batch, run_batch(binary, batch, directory, test_env))
            return
        nextest, package, threads = runner
        safe = [name for name in names if NEXTEST_SAFE_NAME.fullmatch(name)]
        for batch in test_batches(
                safe, BOXED_NEXTEST_BATCH if deadline is not None else NEXTEST_BATCH_MAX_TESTS,
                NEXTEST_BATCH_MAX_BYTES):
            if out_of_time():
                return
            settle(batch, run_nextest_batch(nextest, package, target, batch, directory, test_env, threads))
        safe_set = set(safe)
        for batch in test_batches([name for name in names if name not in safe_set]):
            if out_of_time():
                return
            settle(batch, run_batch(binary, batch, directory, test_env))

    lists = interleave(tier, 1 if runner else shards)
    if len(lists) == 1:
        shard(lists[0])
    else:
        errors = []

        def guarded(names):
            try:
                shard(names)
            except BaseException as error:  # a shard crash must fail the run, not vanish
                errors.append(error)
                failed[0] = True

        threads = [threading.Thread(target=guarded, args=(names,), daemon=True) for names in lists]
        for thread in threads:
            thread.start()
        for thread in threads:
            thread.join()
        if errors:
            raise errors[0]
    return total[0], failed[0], expired[0]


def run_selected(plan, artifacts, test_env, exact_tests=None):
    failed, count = False, 0
    # #2270: the released-migration tests the diff does not need (check-touched-shards decided).
    slow_names, slow_skipped = set(plan.get("slow_tier_excluded") or ()), set()
    ignored_only = set()  # #2273: filters whose every match is #[ignore]d
    box = plan.get("time_box_sec")
    started = time.monotonic()
    deadline = started + box if box else None
    selected_tests, run_tests, expired = 0, 0, False
    changed_selected, changed_run = 0, 0  # tests of a changed module (#2182)
    direct_selected, direct_run = 0, 0  # proven direct observers of a stopped broad trace (#2588)
    coverage = {}  # filter -> [selected test identities, executed ones] in this package (#216)
    bounded, deferred_tests = {}, 0  # cap-overflow filter -> [kept, executed, deferred]; deferred identities (#2592)
    # Every binary is listed and ranked first; the tiers of all of them then run in one rank order,
    # so a binary listed earlier cannot spend the box before another binary's changed-module or
    # direct-observer tests (#2588).
    records = []
    for binary, (target, directory) in artifacts.items():
        selections = [s for s in plan["selections"]
                      if s["kind"] in target["kind"] and (s["kind"] == "lib" or s["target"] == target["name"])]
        if not selections:
            continue
        listing = []
        result = stream([binary, "--list", "--format=terse"], listing.append, own_tree=False, cwd=directory)
        if result:
            print("".join(listing), end="", flush=True)
            print("SCOPED_PHASE list_failed", flush=True)
            return result
        names = [line[:-len(": test\n")] for line in listing if line.endswith(": test\n")]
        # #2273: `--list` also names #[ignore]d tests, which nextest then skips ("no tests to
        # run"). Like the lander (rsi-rolling-land `listed_selected_tests`: matching and not
        # ignored), leave them out; `--list --ignored` is the libtest way to name that set.
        ignored = []
        if names and stream([binary, "--list", "--ignored", "--format=terse"], ignored.append,
                            own_tree=False, cwd=directory) == 0:
            ignored = {line[:-len(": test\n")] for line in ignored if line.endswith(": test\n")}
        else:
            ignored = set()
        selected, selection_sizes, by_filter, priority_tests = set(), {}, {}, set()
        bounded_matches = {}  # a cap-overflow module filter's matches, restricted below (#2592)
        direct_only = set()  # proven direct observers a bounded plan selects in their own right (#2592)
        for selection in selections:
            if selection.get("regex"):
                matches = {name for name in names if re.search(selection["regex"], name)}
            elif selection.get("exact"):
                matches = {name for name in names if name == selection["exact"]}
            else:
                matches = {name for name in names if selection["substring"] in name}
            if exact_tests is not None:
                matches &= set(exact_tests)
            if selection.get("direct_only"):
                # Not a filter of the plan: no coverage, no required match, no ignored-only note.
                direct_only |= matches - ignored - (slow_names if selection["kind"] == "lib" else set())
                continue
            if matches and matches <= ignored:
                # Matched, but only ignored tests: nothing to run, and not an unmatched filter.
                ignored_only.add(selection["filter"])
                print("SCOPED_MATCH " + selection["filter"], flush=True)
                continue
            matches -= ignored
            skipped = matches & slow_names if selection["kind"] == "lib" else set()
            if skipped:
                slow_skipped |= skipped
                matches -= skipped
                if not matches:
                    # The filter selects tests, all in the slow tier: matched, nothing to run.
                    print("SCOPED_MATCH " + selection["filter"], flush=True)
                    continue
            if matches and selection.get("bounded"):
                print("SCOPED_MATCH " + selection["filter"], flush=True)
                bounded_matches.setdefault(selection["filter"], set()).update(matches)
                continue
            if matches:
                print("SCOPED_MATCH " + selection["filter"], flush=True)
                selected.update(matches)
                if selection.get("priority"):
                    priority_tests.update(matches)
                by_filter.setdefault(selection["filter"], set()).update(matches)
                for name in matches:
                    selection_sizes[name] = min(selection_sizes.get(name, len(matches)), len(matches))
        runner = None
        if plan.get("runner") == "nextest" and plan.get("nextest"):
            runner = (plan["nextest"], plan["package"], plan.get("test_threads") or plan.get("test_shards", 1))
        # Ranks are judged over everything the plan's filters match, bounded matches included, so a
        # restriction can never make a module that selects more than the limit look small (#2592).
        selected |= direct_only
        for test in direct_only:
            selection_sizes.setdefault(test, 1)
        universe = selected.union(*bounded_matches.values()) if bounded_matches else selected
        changed_ranks = changed_module_ranks(universe, plan.get("changed_modules") or (), target)
        direct_ranks = direct_observer_ranks(universe, [s for s in plan.get("direct_selections") or ()
                                                        if s["kind"] in target["kind"]
                                                        and (s["kind"] == "lib" or s["target"] == target["name"])])
        kept_by_bounded = {}
        for name, matches in bounded_matches.items():
            # Only the changed module's own tests and the proven direct observers run; nothing else the
            # module filter matches is selected, whatever the box would still admit (#2592).
            kept = {test for test in matches if test in changed_ranks or test in direct_ranks}
            kept_by_bounded[name] = kept
            selected |= kept
            for test in kept:
                selection_sizes[test] = min(selection_sizes.get(test, len(matches)), len(matches))
        deferred = set().union(*bounded_matches.values()) - selected if bounded_matches else set()
        changed_ranks = {test: rank for test, rank in changed_ranks.items() if test in selected}
        direct_ranks = {test: rank for test, rank in direct_ranks.items() if test in selected}
        changed_selected += len(changed_ranks)
        direct_selected += len(direct_ranks)
        records.append({"binary": binary, "target": target, "directory": directory, "runner": runner,
                        "selected": selected, "by_filter": by_filter, "changed": set(changed_ranks),
                        "bounded": {name: (kept_by_bounded[name], matches - selected)
                                    for name, matches in bounded_matches.items()},
                        "deferred": deferred,
                        "direct": set(direct_ranks), "ran": set(),
                        "tiers": ranked_tiers(selected, selection_sizes, priority_tests, changed_ranks, direct_ranks)})
    queue = sorted(((key, index, tier) for index, record in enumerate(records) for key, tier in record["tiers"]),
                   key=lambda item: item[:2])
    for _key, index, tier in queue:
        record = records[index]
        ran, tier_failed, tier_expired = run_tier(
            record["binary"], tier, record["directory"], test_env, record["target"], plan.get("test_shards", 1),
            runner=record["runner"], deadline=deadline, ran_names=record["ran"])
        count += ran
        failed |= tier_failed
        if tier_expired:
            expired = True
            break
    for record in records:
        ran_names, selected = record["ran"], record["selected"]
        selected_tests += len(selected)
        run_tests += len(ran_names & selected) if ran_names else 0
        changed_run += len(ran_names & record["changed"])
        direct_run += len(ran_names & record["direct"])
        for name, matches in record["by_filter"].items():
            seen = coverage.setdefault(name, [0, 0])
            seen[0] += len(matches)
            seen[1] += len(matches & ran_names)
        deferred_tests += len(record["deferred"])
        for name, (kept, left) in record["bounded"].items():
            seen = bounded.setdefault(name, [0, 0, 0])
            seen[0] += len(kept)
            seen[1] += len(kept & ran_names)
            seen[2] += len(left)
    print(f"scoped-test: {plan['package']} executed {count} tests", flush=True)
    for name in sorted(ignored_only):
        print("SCOPED_IGNORED_ONLY " + name, flush=True)
    if slow_skipped:
        print(f"SCOPED_SLOW_TIER_SKIPPED {len(slow_skipped)}", flush=True)
    if box or bounded or any(s.get("bounded") for s in plan["selections"]):
        # Per filter, the selected and executed test identities: a failed test that ran is
        # executed (the verdict stays the exit code), a test the box never started is not. A
        # cap-overflow filter reports kept/executed/deferred apart: deferred is neither run nor passed.
        print("SCOPED_SUBSET " + json.dumps({
            "package": plan["package"], "selected_tests": selected_tests, "run_tests": run_tests,
            "changed_module_tests": [changed_selected, changed_run],
            "direct_observer_tests": [direct_selected, direct_run], "box_expired": expired, "filters": {name: seen for name, seen in sorted(coverage.items())},
            "bounded": {name: seen for name, seen in sorted(bounded.items())}, "deferred_tests": deferred_tests,
            "test_sec": round(time.monotonic() - started, 1)}), flush=True)
    # Every binary built and listed: only now may a missing filter mean "no tests".
    print("SCOPED_PHASE listed", flush=True)
    if failed:
        print("SCOPED_PHASE test_failed", flush=True)
        return 1
    # A shared filter may match only in another package. The parent checks
    # that every filter selects something across the complete package union.
    print(f"SCOPED_COMPLETE {plan['package']} {count}", flush=True)
    return 0


BUILD_ENV_NAMES = (
    "PATH", "CARGO_TARGET_DIR", "CARGO_BUILD_JOBS", "RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN",
    "RUSTFLAGS", "CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS", "RSI_CARGO_SLOT_HELD", "TMPDIR", "TMP", "TEMP", "RUST_BACKTRACE",
    "CHECK_TOUCHED_SHARDS_TMPDIR",
    "CARGO_INCREMENTAL", "CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG", "RUSTC_WRAPPER", "RSI_BUILD_SLOTS",
    "RSI_BUILD_SLOT_ROOT", "RSI_BUILD_SCCACHE", "SCCACHE_CACHE_SIZE", "SCCACHE_DIR",
    "SCCACHE_SERVER_UDS", "SCCACHE_CLIENT_SIDE",
    # cargo-slot sets these for its child; the slot is held outside the unit now (#1756).
    "RUST_TEST_THREADS", "NEXTEST_TEST_THREADS",
)


class BuildAdmission:
    """Hold one cargo-slot lease while the parent runs bounded package units.

    The holder waits for EOF on its stdin. Normal exit, exceptions and signals
    close that pipe, so cargo-slot releases its lease (or its local flock). Only
    build knobs cross back from the holder; authority never crosses into units.
    """

    PREFIX = "SCOPED_SLOT_ENV "

    def __init__(self):
        self.process = None
        self.environment = {}
        self.previous = {}

    def __enter__(self):
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *_exc):
        self.close()

    def close(self):
        if self.process is not None:
            self.process.stdin.close()
            try:
                self.process.wait(timeout=5)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                stop_process(self.process)
            self.reader.join(timeout=5)
            self.process = None
        for name, value in self.previous.items():
            if value is None:
                os.environ.pop(name, None)
            else:
                os.environ[name] = value
        self.previous.clear()

    def acquire(self, slot, emit, budget, wait_cap, job_deadline):
        if self.process is not None:
            if self.process.poll() is not None:
                raise OSError("shared build-slot holder exited before the package run finished")
            return 0
        command = [*slot, sys.executable, "-u", str(SCRIPT), "--hold-admission"]
        env = dict(os.environ)
        if job_deadline is not None:
            env["RSI_CARGO_ADMISSION_MAX_SEC"] = str(max(1, math.ceil(wait_cap)))
        self.process = subprocess.Popen(command, stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                        stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, errors="replace",
                                        start_new_session=os.name != "nt", env=env)
        lines = queue.Queue()

        def read():
            try:
                for line in self.process.stdout:
                    lines.put(line)
            finally:
                self.process.stdout.close()
                lines.put(None)

        self.reader = threading.Thread(target=read, daemon=True)
        self.reader.start()
        try:
            while True:
                try:
                    line = lines.get(timeout=budget.remaining())
                except queue.Empty:
                    raise subprocess.TimeoutExpired(command, wait_cap)
                if line is None:
                    return self.process.wait(timeout=budget.remaining()) or 1
                if line.startswith(self.PREFIX):
                    environment = json.loads(line[len(self.PREFIX):])
                    if environment.get("RSI_CARGO_SLOT_HELD") != "1":
                        raise OSError("build-slot holder did not attest a held admission")
                    self.environment = {name: value for name, value in environment.items()
                                        if name in BUILD_ENV_NAMES}
                    self.previous = {name: os.environ.get(name) for name in self.environment}
                    os.environ.update(self.environment)
                    return 0
                budget.observe(line)
                emit(line)
        except BaseException:
            self.close()
            raise


def hold_admission():
    # cargo-slot owns the policy and lease; this child only keeps it alive.
    print(BuildAdmission.PREFIX + json.dumps({name: os.environ[name] for name in BUILD_ENV_NAMES
                                             if name in os.environ}), flush=True)
    sys.stdin.read()
    return 0


# #2119: every cargo build and test unit RSI starts runs in this user slice, whose CPUQuota is the
# hub-wide build budget; the daemon configures it (operator setting hub_build_cpu_percent).
BUILD_SLICE = "rsi-build.slice"


def build_slice():
    """The slice name for bounded units; `RSI_BUILD_SLICE=` (empty) opts out."""
    return os.environ.get("RSI_BUILD_SLICE", BUILD_SLICE)


def bounded_command(plan, plan_path, seconds, cpu_quota, linux, env=None):
    """`env` overrides build settings for the unit (the baseline's own target)."""
    env = env or {}
    worker = [sys.executable, "-u", str(SCRIPT), "--execute-plan", str(plan_path)]
    if not linux:
        return worker, None
    # The daemon's job id (RSI_JOB_ID, a UUID) goes in the name so the CPU
    # andon attributes this separate cgroup to its job (#2127).
    job_id = os.environ.get("RSI_JOB_ID", "")
    job_tag = job_id + "-" if re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{8}(-[0-9a-f]{4}){3}-[0-9a-f]{12}", job_id) else ""
    unit = "rsi-scoped-test-" + job_tag + uuid.uuid4().hex
    # Copy only build/runtime knobs; never transport authority or credentials.
    # The unit builds with the worker shell's build settings (#1741): a
    # different profile, incremental mode or compiler wrapper would build a
    # second copy of every artifact in the same target, and a target seeded
    # from the warm pool (scripts/target-pool) needs RUSTC_WRAPPER.
    environment = ["--setenv=" + (f"{name}={env[name]}" if name in env else name)
                   for name in BUILD_ENV_NAMES if name in os.environ or name in env]
    slice_name = build_slice()
    return ["systemd-run", "--user", "--collect", "--wait", "--pipe", "--service-type=exec",
            *(["--slice=" + slice_name] if slice_name else []),
            "--unit=" + unit, "--working-directory=" + plan["repo"],
            "-p", f"RuntimeMaxSec={seconds:g}", "-p", f"CPUQuota={cpu_quota:g}%",
            "-p", "TimeoutStopSec=2", "-p", "KillMode=control-group",
            *environment, *worker], unit


def slice_budget_percent(linux, run=subprocess.run):
    """The build slice's live `CPUQuota` in percent of one CPU, or None (#2524).

    The job's own `--cpu-quota` is the daemon's ceiling; the host's build budget
    is the slice's quota. Nested units run in that slice, so they never ask for
    more than it grants, even when the job was started with a larger ceiling or
    the budget was lowered while it ran. Unknown (no systemd, no slice, no
    quota) means no extra bound.
    """
    name = build_slice()
    if not linux or not name:
        return None
    try:
        shown = run(["systemctl", "--user", "show", name, "-p", "CPUQuotaPerSecUSec", "--value"],
                    capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    text = (shown.stdout or "").strip() if shown.returncode == 0 else ""
    found = re.fullmatch(r"(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)(s|ms|us|min)?", text)
    if not found:
        return None  # "infinity" or empty: the slice carries no quota
    unit = {"us": 1e-6, "ms": 1e-3, "s": 1.0, "min": 60.0, None: 1.0}[found[2]]
    return float(found[1]) * unit * 100


def split_slot(cargo):
    """Separate a leading `cargo-slot` from the cargo command (#1756).

    The slot is acquired once, around all bounded package units, so the time spent
    queued for it is outside the run budget and the unit's RuntimeMaxSec. The held
    slot marks its child (RSI_CARGO_SLOT_HELD), so the build inside takes no second.
    """
    if cargo and Path(cargo[0]).name == "cargo-slot":
        return [cargo[0]], list(cargo[1:])
    return [], list(cargo)


def positive(value):
    number = float(value)
    if not 0 < number < float("inf"):
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError("must be positive and finite")
    return number


def reclaim_incremental(repo):
    """Remove only Cargo's disposable incremental cache for this target tree."""
    target_dir = Path(os.environ.get("CARGO_TARGET_DIR", Path(repo) / "target"))
    if not target_dir.is_absolute():
        target_dir = Path(repo) / target_dir
    cache = target_dir / "debug" / "incremental"
    if not cache.is_dir():
        return 0
    reclaimed = sum(path.stat().st_size for path in cache.rglob("*") if path.is_file())
    shutil.rmtree(cache)
    return reclaimed


def static_guards(repo, base, head, audits):
    """Reuse completed derivation audits; narrowing a plan must not skip guards."""
    guards = RECEIPT["static_guards"] = {}
    commands = {
        # The lander's first guard (`git diff --check <target> <candidate>`); a stack that is
        # green here must not be refused for whitespace before any test runs (#3060).
        "whitespace": ["git", "diff", "--check", base, head],
        "shard_gates": [sys.executable, "scripts/check-rsid-test-shards.py", "--require-gates"],
        "released_migrations": [sys.executable, "tools/check-released-migrations.py", base, head],
        # A starter JSON change moves its digest fixture and SHIPPED_BEFORE together (#3070).
        "starter_pins": [sys.executable, "tools/check_starter_pins.py", base, head],
        "identity_assertions": [sys.executable, "tools/check_identity_assertions.py"],
        "portable_shell": [sys.executable, "tools/check_portable_shell.py", "--base", base],
    }
    failed = False
    for name, command in commands.items():
        audit = audits.get(name, {})
        if audit.get("verdict") in ("ok", "fail"):
            status, detail = audit["verdict"], audit.get("detail", "")
        elif command[0] == sys.executable and not (Path(repo) / command[1]).is_file():
            # The tool copy comes from rolling but a guard script comes from the candidate tree. A
            # guard newer than the candidate's merge base is absent there, and failing it would end
            # the run before any test with no evidence (#3126). The merge queue runs every guard on
            # the merged tree, which does carry it.
            status = "skipped"
            detail = f"{command[1]} not in candidate; merge queue runs it on the merged tree"
        else:
            print("static guard: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
            try:
                result = subprocess.run(command, cwd=repo, text=True, capture_output=True)
                status = "ok" if result.returncode == 0 else "fail"
                detail = (result.stdout + result.stderr).strip()
            except OSError as error:
                status, detail = "fail", str(error)
        guards[name] = {"status": status, "detail": detail}
        print(f"scoped-test: static guard {name}={status}" + (f": {detail}" if detail else ""), flush=True)
        failed |= status not in ("ok", "skipped")
    return failed


# The compiler-warning gate: a crate with a checked-in baseline may not gain warnings (#1897).
WARNING_BASELINE = "tools/warning-baseline.json"
WARNING_NOISE = re.compile(r"(?:\d+ warnings? emitted|aborting due to .*)")
WARNING_SUPPRESSION = re.compile(
    r"(?:^|\s)(?:-A|--allow)[\s=]?(?:warnings|unused[a-z_]*|dead_code)(?=\s|$)|(?:^|\s)--cap-lints\b")
WARNING_LIST_CAP = 20


def warning_package_name(package_id):
    """The Cargo package name in a package id, or None: `path+file:///x/crates/NAME#VERSION`,
    `path+file:///x/crates/DIR#NAME@VERSION` and the old `NAME VERSION (SOURCE)` forms."""
    text = str(package_id or "")
    if "#" in text:
        source, _, spec = text.rpartition("#")
        name = spec.partition("@")[0]
        if name and not name[0].isdigit():
            return name
        return source.rstrip("/").rsplit("/", 1)[-1] or None
    return text.split(" ", 1)[0] or None


def warning_suppression(env):
    """Why this build cannot show every warning (flags that allow them), else None."""
    flags = " ".join(str(env.get(name, "")).replace("\x1f", " ")
                     for name in ("RUSTFLAGS", "CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS", "CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS"))
    hit = WARNING_SUPPRESSION.search(flags)
    return f"compiler flags suppress warnings ({hit.group(0).strip()})" if hit else None


class WarningCollector:
    """Compiler warnings of one supervised build, from the JSON diagnostics Cargo already streams.

    A warning is attributed to its Cargo package (`package_id`) and keyed by file, lint code and
    message, never by line: a position only tells two reports of the same warning apart, so the
    lib and lib-test units that both report one warning count it once. `measured` names, per
    package, the units Cargo built (artifacts), so a package this run did not build is never
    read as clean. Anything that makes the diagnostics untrustworthy lands in `degraded`."""

    def __init__(self, root, env=None):
        self.root = str(root).replace("\\", "/").rstrip("/") + "/"
        self.measured, self.positions, self.unattributed, self.degraded = {}, {}, 0, []
        reason = warning_suppression(os.environ if env is None else env)
        if reason:
            self.degraded.append(reason)

    def artifact(self, message):
        package = warning_package_name(message.get("package_id"))
        kinds = (message.get("target") or {}).get("kind") or ["?"]
        label = str(kinds[0]) + (" test" if (message.get("profile") or {}).get("test") else "")
        if package:
            self.measured.setdefault(package, set()).add(label)

    def diagnostic(self, message):
        detail = message.get("message") or {}
        text = str(detail.get("message", ""))
        if detail.get("level") != "warning" or WARNING_NOISE.fullmatch(text):
            return
        package = warning_package_name(message.get("package_id"))
        if not package:
            self.unattributed += 1
            return
        spans = detail.get("spans") or []
        span = next((item for item in spans if item.get("is_primary")), spans[0] if spans else {})
        name = str(span.get("file_name", "")).replace("\\", "/")
        name = name[len(self.root):] if name.startswith(self.root) else name
        code = (detail.get("code") or {}).get("code") or ""
        position = f"{span.get('line_start', 0)}:{span.get('column_start', 0)}"
        self.positions.setdefault(package, {}).setdefault(f"{name}|{code}|{text}", set()).add(position)

    def text(self, line):
        """A line that is not JSON. Cargo's own notes are fine; a rendered rustc location
        (` --> file:line:col`) is a diagnostic that bypassed the JSON stream, so it is not evidence."""
        if re.match(r"\s*--> \S+:\d+:\d+", line) and "rendered diagnostic" not in " ".join(self.degraded):
            self.degraded.append("a rendered diagnostic arrived outside the JSON stream")

    def payload(self):
        return {"measured": {name: sorted(labels) for name, labels in sorted(self.measured.items())},
                "warnings": {name: {key: sorted(places) for key, places in sorted(keys.items())}
                             for name, keys in sorted(self.positions.items())},
                "unattributed": self.unattributed, "degraded": self.degraded}


def merge_warning_payloads(payloads):
    """One view over every package build of a run: positions and configs are unions, so a unit
    several `cargo test -p` builds report again is counted once."""
    merged = {"measured": {}, "warnings": {}, "unattributed": 0, "degraded": []}
    for payload in payloads:
        for name, labels in (payload.get("measured") or {}).items():
            merged["measured"][name] = sorted(set(merged["measured"].get(name, [])) | set(labels))
        for name, keys in (payload.get("warnings") or {}).items():
            into = merged["warnings"].setdefault(name, {})
            for key, places in keys.items():
                into[key] = sorted(set(into.get(key, [])) | set(places))
        merged["unattributed"] += int(payload.get("unattributed") or 0)
        merged["degraded"] += [item for item in payload.get("degraded") or [] if item not in merged["degraded"]]
    return merged


def read_warning_baseline(text):
    """Parse the checked-in baseline; a ValueError names the defect (a broken file never disables the gate)."""
    document = json.loads(text)
    packages = document.get("packages") if isinstance(document, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(packages, dict) or document.get("schema") != 1:
        raise ValueError('expected {"schema": 1, "packages": {NAME: {"configs": [...], "warnings": {KEY: COUNT}}}}')
    for name, entry in packages.items():
        warnings = entry.get("warnings") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None
        configs = entry.get("configs") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None
        if (not isinstance(warnings, dict) or not isinstance(configs, list) or not configs or
                any(not isinstance(key, str) or isinstance(count, bool) or not isinstance(count, int) or count < 1
                    for key, count in warnings.items())):
            raise ValueError(f"package {name}: needs a non-empty `configs` list and `warnings` counts of 1 or more")
    return document


def base_warning_baseline(repo, base, run=subprocess.run):
    """The baseline at `base`: (document, state), state present | absent | unresolved | unreadable."""
    def git(*args):
        return run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *args], text=True, capture_output=True)
    try:
        if git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", base + "^{commit}").returncode:
            return None, "unresolved"
        shown = git("show", f"{base}:{WARNING_BASELINE}")
    except OSError:
        return None, "unresolved"
    if shown.returncode:
        return None, "absent"
    try:
        return read_warning_baseline(shown.stdout), "present"
    except ValueError:
        return None, "unreadable"


def evaluate_warning_gate(observed, candidate, base, base_state):
    """The verdict for one run. Pure: observed payload, the candidate's baseline, the base's (or None).

    ok               every measured baselined package is at or under its baseline
    new_warnings     a measured package has a warning key above its baseline count
    baseline_raised  the candidate's baseline is above the base's, or drops a package the base has
    unavailable      the diagnostics cannot be trusted (suppressed, unparsed or unattributed)
    not_measured     no baselined package was built in this run (nothing to compare)
    """
    packages, refusals = {}, []
    for name, entry in sorted(candidate["packages"].items()):
        before = ((base or {}).get("packages") or {}).get(name)
        if before is not None:
            for key, count in sorted(entry["warnings"].items()):
                if count > before["warnings"].get(key, 0):
                    refusals.append(f"{name}: baseline raised for {key!r}: "
                                    f"{before['warnings'].get(key, 0)} -> {count}")
    if base is not None:
        for name in sorted(set(base["packages"]) - set(candidate["packages"])):
            refusals.append(f"{name}: its baseline was removed; lower the counts instead")
    unavailable = list(observed.get("degraded") or [])
    if observed.get("unattributed"):
        unavailable.append(f"{observed['unattributed']} warning(s) carried no package id")
    for name, entry in sorted(candidate["packages"].items()):
        configs = observed["measured"].get(name)
        if configs is None:
            packages[name] = {"status": "not_measured", "baseline": sum(entry["warnings"].values())}
            continue
        counts = {key: len(places) for key, places in (observed["warnings"].get(name) or {}).items()}
        new = [(key, counts[key] - entry["warnings"].get(key, 0)) for key in sorted(counts)
               if counts[key] > entry["warnings"].get(key, 0)]
        complete = set(entry["configs"]) <= set(configs)
        fixed = ([(key, count - counts.get(key, 0)) for key, count in sorted(entry["warnings"].items())
                  if counts.get(key, 0) < count] if complete else [])
        packages[name] = {"status": "new_warnings" if new else "ok", "observed": sum(counts.values()),
                          "baseline": sum(entry["warnings"].values()), "configs": configs, "complete": complete,
                          "new": [{"key": key, "count": count} for key, count in new[:WARNING_LIST_CAP]],
                          "new_total": sum(count for _, count in new),
                          "fixed": [{"key": key, "count": count} for key, count in fixed[:WARNING_LIST_CAP]],
                          "fixed_total": sum(count for _, count in fixed)}
    measured = [item for item in packages.values() if item["status"] != "not_measured"]
    if refusals:
        status = "baseline_raised"
    elif measured and unavailable:
        status = "unavailable"
    elif any(item["status"] == "new_warnings" for item in measured):
        status = "new_warnings"
    else:
        status = "ok" if measured else "not_measured"
    summary = {"status": status, "packages": packages, "base_baseline": base_state}
    if refusals:
        summary["refusals"] = refusals
    if unavailable and measured:
        summary["unavailable"] = unavailable
    return summary


def warning_gate(repo, base, payloads, run=subprocess.run):
    """Judge a run's diagnostics against `tools/warning-baseline.json`, or None when the repository has none."""
    path = Path(repo) / WARNING_BASELINE
    if not path.is_file():
        return None
    try:
        candidate = read_warning_baseline(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError) as error:
        return {"status": "unavailable", "unavailable": [f"{WARNING_BASELINE} is unreadable: {error}"],
                "packages": {}}
    document, state = base_warning_baseline(repo, base, run)
    return evaluate_warning_gate(merge_warning_payloads(payloads), candidate, document, state)


def report_warning_gate(gate):
    """Print the verdict; True when it fails the run."""
    for name, item in sorted(gate["packages"].items()):
        if item["status"] == "not_measured":
            print(f"scoped-test: warnings {name}: not built in this run (baseline {item['baseline']})", flush=True)
            continue
        print(f"scoped-test: warnings {name}: {item['observed']} observed, baseline {item['baseline']}, "
              f"{item['new_total']} new (units: {', '.join(item['configs'])})", flush=True)
        for entry in item["new"]:
            print(f"scoped-test: new warning x{entry['count']}: {entry['key']}", flush=True)
        if item["fixed"]:
            print(f"scoped-test: warnings {name}: {item['fixed_total']} baseline warning(s) are gone; "
                  f"lower {WARNING_BASELINE}", flush=True)
        elif not item["complete"]:
            print(f"scoped-test: warnings {name}: the baseline names units this run did not build; "
                  "no baseline warning is reported as fixed", flush=True)
    for text in gate.get("refusals", []):
        print(f"scoped-test: warning_baseline_raised: {text}", flush=True)
    for text in gate.get("unavailable", []):
        print(f"scoped-test: warning_evidence_unavailable: {text}", flush=True)
    failed = gate["status"] in ("new_warnings", "baseline_raised", "unavailable")
    if failed:
        print(f"scoped-test: warning_gate_failed: {gate['status']}", flush=True)
    return failed


def baseline_from_log(lines):
    """A `packages` document for every package a job log measured (`SCOPED_WARNINGS` lines), to seed
    or lower `tools/warning-baseline.json`: configs are the units seen, counts the distinct warnings."""
    payloads = []
    for line in lines:
        if line.startswith("SCOPED_WARNINGS "):
            payloads.append(json.loads(line.split(" ", 1)[1]))
    merged = merge_warning_payloads(payloads)
    return {"schema": 1, "packages": {
        name: {"configs": configs,
               "warnings": {key: len(places) for key, places in sorted((merged["warnings"].get(name) or {}).items())}}
        for name, configs in sorted(merged["measured"].items())}}


# The typed receipt the daemon's scoped-test job reads from the log tail (#1638).
RECEIPT = {"schema": 1, "emit": False, "packages": []}
RECEIPT_PREFIX = "SCOPED_TEST_RECEIPT "


def planning_compile(explicit, dry_run):
    """The truthful compile label of a plan (#2564): planning never starts cargo; the build and test
    compile happen later, inside this admitted job, and the receipt says whether they did."""
    return {"planning": {"source": "explicit_filters" if explicit else "filters_only", "cargo_invoked": False},
            "execution": "skipped_dry_run" if dry_run else "pending", "packages": {}}


def package_compile_status(phases):
    """How one package's supervised build went, from its SCOPED_PHASE lines."""
    if "build_failed" in phases:
        return "failed"
    if phases.intersection({"listed", "list_failed", "test_failed"}):
        return "executed"
    return "incomplete"


def slow_tier_plan(checker, repo, head, derived, explicit):
    """(receipt entry, excluded test names) for the slow tier of the released-migration tests (#2270).

    Explicit filters run exactly what they name. A derived plan excludes the tier unless
    check-touched-shards (the one place that reads the diff) decided `full`: a trigger path or a
    line of a slow test's own source changed, no config, or an unreadable decision."""
    decision = {"mode": "full", "reason": "explicit_filters"} if explicit else dict(
        derived.get("slow_tier") or {"mode": "full", "reason": "no_decision"})
    entry = {"mode": decision.get("mode", "full"), "reason": decision.get("reason"), "skipped_tests": 0,
             "package": None}
    for key in ("path", "line"):
        if key in decision:
            entry[key] = decision[key]
    if entry["mode"] != "excluded":
        return entry, []
    try:
        config = checker["load_slow_tier"](repo, head)
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError) as error:
        entry.update(mode="full", reason=f"config_unreadable: {error}")
        return entry, []
    if config is None:
        entry.update(mode="full", reason="no_slow_tier_config")
        return entry, []
    entry.update(package=config["package"], tests=len(config["tests"]))
    return entry, sorted(config["tests"])


def slow_tier_summary(entry):
    if entry["mode"] == "excluded":
        return (f"slow tier excluded ({entry['reason']}): {entry.get('tests', 0)} released-migration tests are "
                "left to the merge-queue full QA; a diff that touches a migration, the runner or such a test runs them")
    where = f" at {entry['path']}" if entry.get("path") else ""
    return f"slow tier runs in full ({entry['reason']}{where})"


def direct_observer_plan(derived, shard_packages):
    """{package: selections} of the proven direct observers of every stopped broad trace (#2588).

    They are an ordering hint only and never part of `plan`: a test they name runs because a plan
    filter selects it, so the receipt's filter counts and executed identities are those of the plan."""
    items = [i for i in derived.get("direct_observer_filters") or () if isinstance(i, dict) and i.get("filter")]
    if not items:
        return {}
    distances = {i["filter"]: int(i.get("distance") or 1) for i in items}
    plan = selection_plan(list(distances), shard_packages)
    for selections in plan.values():
        for selection in selections:
            selection["distance"] = distances[selection["filter"]]
    summary = derived.get("direct_observers") or {}
    RECEIPT["direct_observers"] = {"filters": len(items), "tests": summary.get("tests"),
                                   "by_distance": summary.get("by_distance")}
    print(f"scoped-test: direct observers of the stopped broad traces run nearest first, before the "
          f"unranked far observers of any specificity tier: {summary.get('tests', '?')} tests, "
          f"{len(items)} filters, by distance {summary.get('by_distance')}", flush=True)
    return plan


def note_broad_observers(derived):
    """Record a broad observer fallback in the receipt instead of refusing (#2105).

    check-touched-shards keeps a module filter when the observer trace is too wide to name
    (more than 150 functions or 24 tests) and says so in `broad_filters`. The plan then runs
    as a bounded targeted subset; the uncertainty stays visible for the manager.
    """
    items = [item for item in derived.get("broad_filters") or [] if isinstance(item, dict)]
    if not (derived.get("broad") or items):
        return
    selections = distinct_expressions(items)
    RECEIPT.update(uncertain="broad_observers", broad_filter_count=len(selections),
                   broad_filter_entries=len(items), broad_tests=sum(selections.values()),
                   broad_filters=[{key: item[key] for key in ("filter", "tests", "reason", "direct_tests", "trace_layers",
                                                              "trace_stopped", "hubs") if key in item}
                                  for item in items])
    print(f"scoped-test: uncertain: broad_observers ({len(selections)} filters, "
          f"{RECEIPT['broad_tests']} tests); the bounded subset below is not the whole observer "
          "set, the merge-queue gate and the full-library QA cover the rest", flush=True)


DEFERRED_ROUTES = ("merge_queue_gate", "pinned_full_qa")


def distinct_expressions(entries):
    """{filter expression: its tests}: one selection per expression, whatever shard labels carry it.

    A shard label only routes a filter (#1698): the same expression under two labels selects the
    same tests, so the derivation's per-label count (`/^store::/` under `other-01` and `store-01`,
    2189 each) is one selection counted twice. Only an identical expression is merged; a different
    expression, however similar its count, stays its own entry."""
    expressions = {}
    for item in entries:
        expression = str(item.get("filter", "")).partition(":test(")[2]
        expressions[expression] = max(expressions.get(expression, 0), int(item.get("tests") or 0))
    return expressions


def scope_tests_at_most(entries):
    """The most tests the cap-overflow module filters can select, each identity counted once.

    The derivation counts a filter's tests from the source inventory: the same expression under two
    shard labels is one selection, and an anchored `^module::` filter inside another is covered by
    it. Other filters add up, so this is an upper bound, not the deferred count: the changed-module
    tests and the direct observers a run keeps are still inside it."""
    expressions = distinct_expressions(entries)
    anchored = {re.fullmatch(r"/\^([\w:]+)::/\)", expression)[1]: expression
                for expression in expressions if re.fullmatch(r"/\^([\w:]+)::/\)", expression)}
    covered = {expression for module, expression in anchored.items()
               if any(module.startswith(other + "::") for other in anchored)}
    return sum(tests for expression, tests in expressions.items() if expression not in covered)


def bounded_worker_selection(derived):
    """The worker's bounded view of a derivation whose traces stopped at a cap, or None (#2592).

    check-touched-shards keeps the module filter in `suggested_filters` when a trace is too wide
    to name: that list stays the merge-queue gate's selection. A broad entry it flagged
    `worker_bounded` (a hub, the function bound or the test bound, with every identity placed) is
    not widened for the worker: scoped-test selects only the module's changed-module tests and the
    proven direct observers, and records the rest as deferred to the gate and the pinned full QA.
    A filter any unbounded broad entry (an unresolved identity, an untraceable diff) also produced
    stays a broad worker filter. Named filters the module filter had absorbed (`merged_filters`)
    return to the plan, so a source scanner or catalog pin is never lost with its covering filter."""
    broad = [item for item in derived.get("broad_filters") or () if isinstance(item, dict) and item.get("filter")]
    unbounded = {item["filter"] for item in broad if not item.get("worker_bounded")}
    bounded = {item["filter"] for item in broad if item.get("worker_bounded")} - unbounded
    suggested = list(derived.get("suggested_filters") or ())
    planned = [value for value in suggested if value in bounded]
    if not planned:
        return None
    covered, restored = set(planned), []
    merged = [item for item in derived.get("merged_filters") or () if isinstance(item, dict)]
    settled = False
    while not settled:
        settled = True
        for item in merged:
            value, by = item.get("filter"), item.get("covered_by")
            if not value or by not in covered or value in covered or value in restored:
                continue
            settled = False
            if value in bounded or str(item.get("reason", "")).startswith("identical expression"):
                covered.add(value)
            else:
                restored.append(value)
    entries = [item for item in broad if item.get("filter") in bounded]
    stays = sorted({value for value in unbounded if value in suggested})
    return {"filters": list(dict.fromkeys([*[v for v in suggested if v not in bounded], *restored, *planned])),
            "bounded": planned, "restored": restored, "entries": entries, "broad_filters_left": stays}


def deferred_record(bounded, head, direct):
    """The receipt's `deferred` block before any test ran: what is deferred, why and where it is proven."""
    entries = bounded["entries"]
    stops = sorted({str(item.get("stop")) for item in entries})
    return {
        "policy": "bounded_worker_selection",
        "reason": "the observer trace stopped at a cap (" + ", ".join(stops) + "): the worker runs the changed "
                  "modules' tests and the proven direct observers nearest first and does not widen to the module filters",
        "filters": [{key: item[key] for key in ("filter", "tests", "stop", "reason", "path", "direct_tests", "hubs")
                     if key in item} for item in entries],
        "bounded_filters": list(bounded["bounded"]),
        "restored_filters": list(bounded["restored"]),
        "scope_tests_at_most": scope_tests_at_most(entries),
        "scope_basis": "derived source inventory, before the kept changed-module and direct-observer tests are subtracted",
        "direct_observers": dict(direct or {}),
        "tests": None,
        "tests_basis": "pending: counted from the package test listings when the run lists them",
        "routes": list(DEFERRED_ROUTES),
        "candidate": head,
        "status": "pending",
        "status_basis": "deferred coverage is not passed, run or fully verified here: the merge-queue gate runs "
                        "suggested_filters, the full-library QA runs the whole candidate once it is landed",
    }


def record_deferred(coverage):
    """Fold the executed listing counts into the deferred block (identities, never a pass)."""
    deferred = RECEIPT.get("deferred")
    if not deferred:
        return
    unlisted = coverage["unlisted_packages"]
    deferred.update(tests=coverage["deferred_tests"],
                    tests_basis=("listed test identities no plan filter, changed-module rank or direct-observer rank kept; "
                                 + ("incomplete: " + ", ".join(unlisted) + " never listed" if unlisted
                                    else "every selected package listed")),
                    bounded_coverage=coverage["bounded"][:20], bounded_coverage_total=len(coverage["bounded"]))
    RECEIPT.update(filters_bounded=coverage["bounded"][:20], filters_bounded_total=len(coverage["bounded"]),
                   deferred_tests=coverage["deferred_tests"])


def with_direct_observers(plan, direct_plan):
    """Select the proven direct observers in a bounded plan, in whatever package and binary they sit.

    Ordinarily the direct-observer hints only order tests the plan already selects. A bounded plan
    no longer holds the module filter that selected most of them, and some sit outside every plan
    filter (a scheduler or satellite test that calls the changed function), so each one is selected
    itself and built with its package. These selections are not filters of the plan: they have no
    coverage entry and no required match, and the executed identities count them (#2592)."""
    for package, selections in direct_plan.items():
        plan.setdefault(package, []).extend({**selection, "priority": False, "direct_only": True}
                                            for selection in selections)


def announce_bounded_plan(bounded, plan, head, derived, direct_plan):
    """Mark the cap-overflow filters of `plan` bounded and publish the deferred block (#2592)."""
    marked = set(bounded["bounded"])
    for selections in plan.values():
        for selection in selections:
            if selection["filter"] in marked:
                selection["bounded"] = True
    with_direct_observers(plan, direct_plan)
    deferred = deferred_record(bounded, head, derived.get("direct_observers"))
    RECEIPT.update(worker_policy="bounded_deferred", deferred=deferred,
                   worker_broad_filters=len(bounded["broad_filters_left"]))
    print(f"scoped-test: worker policy: {len(marked)} cap-overflow module filters "
          f"(stopped at {', '.join(sorted({str(i.get('stop')) for i in bounded['entries']}))}) are NOT widened; "
          f"the worker selects their changed-module tests and the {deferred['direct_observers'].get('tests', 0)} proven "
          f"direct observers nearest first, {len(bounded['restored'])} absorbed named filters return to the plan, and "
          f"{len(bounded['broad_filters_left'])} broad worker filters remain", flush=True)
    print(f"scoped-test: deferred (status {deferred['status']}, not run, not passed): up to "
          f"{deferred['scope_tests_at_most']} tests of {len(deferred['filters'])} derived module filters minus the kept "
          f"ones; routed to the {' and the '.join(r.replace('_', ' ') for r in deferred['routes'])} of candidate "
          f"{str(head)[:12]}; the exact deferred count is listed when the run lists the tests", flush=True)


def derive_failure_reasons(derived, code):
    """Why check-touched-shards exited non-zero, from its own receipt, so a red is never silent."""
    reasons = []
    if derived.get("merge_clean") is False:
        reasons.append("candidate does not merge cleanly with the base: merge origin/rolling and retry")
    migrations = derived.get("migrations") or {}
    if migrations.get("numbering_conflict"):
        reasons.append("new migration numbering conflicts with the base head")
    if migrations.get("stray_pre_split"):
        reasons.append("stray pre-split migration file")
    reasons += [f"audit {name} failed: {audit.get('detail')}" for name, audit in
                sorted((derived.get("audits") or {}).items()) if audit.get("verdict") == "fail"]
    runs = derived.get("shards") or {}
    if runs.get("compiled_failed"):
        reasons.append("shard compile failed: " + ", ".join(runs["compiled_failed"]))
    return reasons or [f"check-touched-shards exited {code}"]


def tool_copy():
    """Which copy of this script ran (#1938): path, git blob SHA and where it came from.

    The daemon runs the origin/rolling tip's copy (`rolling`, with its rev) unless the
    candidate edits the tool (`candidate`); a manual run or a fallback is `worktree`.
    """
    data = SCRIPT.read_bytes()
    blob = hashlib.sha1(b"blob %d\0" % len(data) + data).hexdigest()
    copy = {"path": str(SCRIPT), "blob": blob,
            "source": os.environ.get("RSI_SCOPED_TEST_TOOL_SOURCE") or "worktree",
            "rev": os.environ.get("RSI_SCOPED_TEST_TOOL_REV") or None}
    files = tool_files_beside_script(os.environ.get("RSI_SCOPED_TEST_TOOL_FILES"))
    if files is not None:
        copy["files"] = files
    return copy


def tool_files_beside_script(names):
    """The helpers the daemon named (comma-separated file names under scripts/), each with its
    git blob SHA as found beside this script, or `null` when the file is absent there.

    The daemon derives the list from what the tool scripts load beside themselves, so a helper
    a candidate adds shows up in the receipt by name; a missing one is `null`, never skipped
    (hub #3024). `None` when the daemon named no list (a manual run or an older daemon).
    """
    if not names:
        return None
    files = {}
    for name in sorted({item.strip() for item in names.split(",") if item.strip()}):
        path = SCRIPT.parent / name
        if "/" in name or not path.is_file():
            files[name] = None
            continue
        data = path.read_bytes()
        files[name] = hashlib.sha1(b"blob %d\0" % len(data) + data).hexdigest()
    return files


MERGE_CONFLICT_PATH_LIMIT = 32


def merge_onto_rolling(repo, head, rolling="origin/rolling"):
    """Informational trial merge of `head` onto the already-resolved `rolling` ref (fetch-free).

    Returns the receipt fields `merges_cleanly_onto_rolling` (true/false/null), `conflict_paths`
    (first 32, sorted) and, only when null, `merge_check_reason`. Never raises and never
    changes the run's verdict: a green run on a commit that no longer merges stays green."""
    unknown = {"merges_cleanly_onto_rolling": None, "conflict_paths": []}
    try:
        def git(*a):
            return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *a], text=True, capture_output=True, timeout=120)
        for ref in (rolling, head):
            if git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", ref + "^{commit}").returncode:
                return {**unknown, "merge_check_reason": f"{ref} does not resolve to a commit"}
        proc = git("merge-tree", "--write-tree", "--name-only", "--no-messages", rolling, head)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        return {**unknown, "merge_check_reason": f"git merge-tree could not run: {error}"}
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return {"merges_cleanly_onto_rolling": True, "conflict_paths": []}
    if proc.returncode != 1:
        detail = (proc.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ["unknown error"])[0]
        return {**unknown, "merge_check_reason": f"git merge-tree exited {proc.returncode}: {detail}"[:200]}
    # Output: the merged tree id, then the conflicted paths, a blank line and (without --no-messages) messages.
    lines = proc.stdout.splitlines()[1:]
    paths = sorted(set(lines[:lines.index("")] if "" in lines else lines))
    return {"merges_cleanly_onto_rolling": False, "conflict_paths": paths[:MERGE_CONFLICT_PATH_LIMIT],
            "conflict_paths_total": len(paths)}


def record_merge_onto_rolling():
    """Fill the receipt's trial-merge fields at the end of a run that resolved a candidate head."""
    head, repo = RECEIPT.get("head"), RECEIPT.get("candidate_repo")
    RECEIPT.pop("candidate_repo", None)
    if not head or not repo or RECEIPT.get("dry_run"):
        return
    RECEIPT.update(merge_onto_rolling(repo, head))


def emit_receipt(exit_code):
    """Print the one-line JSON receipt last, whatever way the run ended."""
    if not RECEIPT["emit"]:
        return
    receipt = {k: v for k, v in RECEIPT.items() if k not in ("emit", "candidate_repo")}
    receipt.update(exit_code=exit_code, ok=exit_code == 0)
    print(RECEIPT_PREFIX + json.dumps(receipt, separators=(",", ":")), flush=True)


def snapshot_package(entry):
    """Keep the latest package progress in receipts that survive interruption."""
    packages = [item for item in RECEIPT["packages"] if item.get("package") != entry["package"]]
    packages.append(entry)
    RECEIPT["packages"] = packages
    emit_receipt(1)


def baseline_target_dir(repo, log_dir):
    """A Cargo target the baseline comparison owns, never the candidate's (#1898).

    Cargo fingerprints key on the workspace path and package id, so one target
    reused across the candidate and a base checkout leaves dependency artifacts
    built from the other revision's source (a library with a field the candidate
    fixtures lack). Keep the baseline under the assigned target tree (#1963),
    with its own fingerprints and dependencies. A warm-pool seed can hard-link
    into it, and it is removed when the comparison settles.
    """
    candidate = Path(os.environ.get("CARGO_TARGET_DIR", Path(repo) / "target"))
    if not candidate.is_absolute():
        candidate = Path(repo) / candidate
    # Also reclaim abandoned sibling targets from the older layout (#1934).
    sweep_stale_baseline_targets(candidate.parent, f"{candidate.name}-baseline-")
    parent = candidate / "scoped-baselines"
    parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    sweep_stale_baseline_targets(parent, "run-")
    return Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="run-", dir=parent))


# A run removes its baseline target when it settles; a killed or timed-out run
# cannot, so the next one sweeps the leftovers beyond any live run's budget.
STALE_BASELINE_SECONDS = 6 * 3600


def sweep_stale_baseline_targets(parent, prefix, max_age=STALE_BASELINE_SECONDS):
    """Remove abandoned baseline targets so disk use stays bounded (#1934)."""
    now = time.time()
    for path in Path(parent).glob(prefix + "*"):
        try:
            if path.is_dir() and not path.is_symlink() and now - path.stat().st_mtime > max_age:
                shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)
        except OSError:
            continue


def seed_baseline_target(worktree, target, log_dir):
    """Best effort: start the baseline target from the warm pool, never fail on it."""
    pool = SCRIPT.parent / "target-pool"
    if not pool.is_file():
        return
    try:
        seeded = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(pool), "seed", "--sandbox", str(worktree),
                                 "--target", str(target)], cwd=worktree, text=True,
                                capture_output=True, timeout=300)
        detail = (seeded.stdout + seeded.stderr).strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
        detail = f"{type(error).__name__}: {error}"
    (Path(log_dir) / "baseline-seed.log").write_text(detail + "\n")


def failure_records(candidate_failures):
    return [{"package": package, "kind": kind, "target": target, "test": test}
            for package, entries in sorted(candidate_failures.items())
            for kind, target, test in sorted({(x["kind"], x["target"], x["test"]) for x in entries})]


def query_failure_records(repo, test_id, timeout):
    """Look up active records by exact name; a lookup error never means no record exists.

    This is annotation only: a name record does not prove this run matched its signature,
    and neither a record nor its absence establishes that a test failed on the base.
    """
    try:
        if not os.environ.get("RSI_SESSION_TOKEN"):
            # Durable jobs intentionally carry no agent authority. Use the shared
            # classifier's exported snapshot there, as the rolling QA tools do.
            binary = os.environ.get("RSI_KNOWN_FAILURE_BIN") or shutil.which("rsi-known-failure")
            if not binary:
                target = Path(os.environ.get("CARGO_TARGET_DIR", str(Path.home() / ".cargo/shared-target")))
                binary = str(target / "debug/rsi-known-failure")
            result = subprocess.run([binary, "query", test_id], cwd=repo, text=True,
                                    capture_output=True, timeout=timeout)
            # The CLI warns for a stale snapshot. It cannot attest to current absence.
            if result.returncode or result.stderr.strip():
                return {"status": "unavailable", "source": "snapshot",
                        "reason": "known-failure snapshot missing, invalid or stale"}
            records = [json.loads(line) for line in result.stdout.splitlines() if line.strip()]
            if any(not isinstance(record, dict) or record.get("test_id") != test_id for record in records):
                return {"status": "unavailable", "source": "snapshot",
                        "reason": "known-failure snapshot query returned invalid records"}
            return {"status": "recorded" if records else "unknown", "source": "snapshot", "records": records}
        result = subprocess.run(
            ["rsi-rpc", "AgentQueryFailureSignatures", "--params", json.dumps({"test_id": test_id})],
            cwd=repo, text=True, capture_output=True, timeout=timeout)
        payload = json.loads(result.stdout).get("result") if result.returncode == 0 else None
        if not isinstance(payload, dict) or not isinstance(payload.get("records"), list):
            return {"status": "unavailable", "reason": "known-failure query did not return records"}
        records = payload["records"]
        # An invalid signature block can hide a record: do not claim absence in that case.
        if not records and payload.get("malformed_issues"):
            return {"status": "unavailable", "reason": "known-failure query found malformed Issues"}
        return {"status": "recorded" if records else "unknown", "records": records}
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return {"status": "unavailable", "reason": "known-failure query unavailable"}


def annotate_baseline_reds(repo, reds):
    """Keep observed base failures and known-failure record availability separate (#397)."""
    annotations, without_record, cache = [], [], {}
    deadline = time.monotonic() + 10
    for failure in reds:
        name = failure["test"]
        if name not in cache:
            left = deadline - time.monotonic()
            cache[name] = (query_failure_records(repo, name, min(5, left)) if left > 0 else
                           {"status": "unavailable", "reason": "known-failure lookup budget exhausted"})
        record = cache[name]
        annotations.append({**failure, **record})
        if record["status"] == "unknown":
            without_record.append(failure)
            print(f"scoped-test: baseline red without known-failure record: {failure['package']} {name}", flush=True)
        elif record["status"] == "unavailable":
            print(f"scoped-test: baseline red known-failure lookup unavailable: {failure['package']} {name}", flush=True)
    return {"baseline_failure_records": annotations, "baseline_reds_without_record": without_record}


def compare_failures_with_base(repo, base, packages, log_dir, seconds, cpu_quota, linux, cargo, tmpfs=None):
    """Rerun only candidate failures on the base, in a detached worktree."""
    failures = {package: names for package, names in packages.items() if names}
    if not failures:
        return {"status": "not_needed", "base": base, "baseline_reds": []}
    if seconds <= 0:
        return {"status": "incomplete", "base": base, "detail": "no comparison budget remained",
                "baseline_reds": []}
    root = Path(repo)
    worktree = Path(log_dir) / "baseline-worktree"
    added = subprocess.run(["git", "worktree", "add", "--detach", "--quiet", str(worktree), base],
                          cwd=root, text=True, capture_output=True)
    if added.returncode:
        detail = (added.stdout + added.stderr).strip()
        print(f"scoped-test: base comparison unavailable: {detail}", flush=True)
        return {"status": "unavailable", "base": base, "detail": detail, "baseline_reds": []}
    if not worktree.is_dir():
        detail = "git worktree did not create the requested directory"
        print(f"scoped-test: base comparison unavailable: {detail}", flush=True)
        return {"status": "unavailable", "base": base, "detail": detail, "baseline_reds": []}
    baseline_reds, checked = [], []
    baseline_target = baseline_target_dir(root, log_dir)
    build_env = {"CARGO_TARGET_DIR": str(baseline_target)}
    print(f"scoped-test: base comparison builds in its own target {baseline_target}", flush=True)
    try:
        seed_baseline_target(worktree, baseline_target, log_dir)
        for index, (package, failed_tests) in enumerate(sorted(failures.items())):
            if index and seconds <= 0:
                break
            selections = []
            for kind, target in sorted({(entry["kind"], entry["target"]) for entry in failed_tests}):
                selections.append({"filter": f"baseline exact failures {kind}:{target}", "kind": kind,
                                   "target": target if kind != "lib" else "", "substring": "",
                                   "exact": None, "regex": None})
            names = sorted({entry["test"] for entry in failed_tests})
            plan = {"repo": str(worktree), "package": package, "selections": selections,
                    "exact_tests": names, "test_shards": max(1, int(cpu_quota // 100)),
                    "build": build_command(package, selections, cargo)}
            if tmpfs:
                plan["tmpfs"] = tmpfs
            plan_path = Path(log_dir) / f"baseline-{package}.json"
            plan_path.write_text(json.dumps(plan) + "\n")
            command, unit = bounded_command(plan, plan_path, max(1, seconds), cpu_quota, linux,
                                            env=build_env)
            started = time.monotonic()
            output_lines = []
            try:
                def stop_unit():
                    if linux and unit:
                        subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "stop", unit], check=False, timeout=30)

                code = stream(command, output_lines.append, timeout=max(1, seconds),
                              stop=stop_unit if linux else None, cwd=worktree,
                              env={**os.environ, **build_env})
                output = "".join(output_lines)
                result = subprocess.CompletedProcess(command, code)
            except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError) as error:
                output = "".join(output_lines)
                output += f"\nscoped-test: base comparison {type(error).__name__}: {error}\n"
                result = None
            (Path(log_dir) / f"baseline-{package}.log").write_text(output)
            expected = {(entry["kind"], entry["target"], entry["test"]) for entry in failed_tests}
            observed = set()
            for line in output.splitlines():
                match = re.fullmatch(r"SCOPED_FAILURE ([^ :]+):([^ ]+) (.+)", line)
                if match and result is not None and result.returncode != 0:
                    identity = tuple(match.groups())
                    if identity in expected:
                        observed.add(identity)
            # A nonzero exit alone is a build/harness failure, not an observed test failure.
            # A successful run can echo failure-looking output; it is never baseline-red.
            baseline_reds.extend({"package": package, "kind": kind, "target": target, "test": name}
                                 for kind, target, name in sorted(observed))
            finished = any(line.startswith(f"scoped-test: {package} executed ")
                           for line in output.splitlines())
            if finished and result is not None:
                checked.extend({"package": package, **entry} for entry in failed_tests)
            print(f"scoped-test: base {package}: " +
                  ("baseline reds found" if any(x["package"] == package for x in baseline_reds)
                   else "no baseline reds found" if result and result.returncode == 0
                   else "comparison incomplete"), flush=True)
            seconds -= time.monotonic() - started
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(baseline_target, ignore_errors=True)
        subprocess.run(["git", "worktree", "remove", "--force", str(worktree)], cwd=root,
                       stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, check=False)
        subprocess.run(["git", "worktree", "prune"], cwd=root, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                       stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, check=False)
        complete = len(checked) == sum(map(len, failures.values()))
    return {"status": "complete" if complete else "incomplete", "base": base,
            "checked": checked, "baseline_reds": baseline_reds}


def fork_point(repo, head, base):
    """The commit the candidate forked from `base`, or None when it is the tip itself or unknown (#2170)."""
    def rev(*a):
        proc = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *a], text=True, capture_output=True)
        return proc.stdout.strip() if proc.returncode == 0 else ""
    mb, tip = rev("merge-base", head, base), rev("rev-parse", "--verify", base + "^{commit}")
    return mb if mb and tip and mb != tip else None


def split_stale_base_failures(repo, head, base, base_failures, comparison, log_dir, seconds,
                              cpu_quota, linux, cargo, tmpfs=None):
    """Failures the tip does not reproduce may be inherited from the candidate's fork point and fixed
    on `base` since. Re-run just those at the fork point (#2170): the ones red there are not new.
    Returns (stale_base_reds, extra) where `extra` is receipt detail."""
    if comparison.get("status") != "complete":
        return [], {}
    tip_reds = {(r["package"], r["kind"], r["target"], r["test"]) for r in comparison["baseline_reds"]}
    pending = {}
    for package, names in base_failures.items():
        rest = [n for n in names if (package, n["kind"], n["target"], n["test"]) not in tip_reds]
        if rest:
            pending[package] = rest
    point = fork_point(repo, head, base) if pending else None
    if not point:
        return [], {}
    sub = Path(log_dir) / "fork-point"
    sub.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    check = compare_failures_with_base(repo, point, pending, sub, seconds, cpu_quota, linux, cargo, tmpfs)
    stale = check.get("baseline_reds", []) if check.get("status") == "complete" else []
    return stale, {"merge_base": point, "status": check.get("status"), "checked": check.get("checked", [])}


def filter_coverage(plan, subsets):
    """Per-filter coverage from the executed selected test identities each package reported (#216).

    A package's SCOPED_SUBSET names, per filter, how many tests the filter selected there and how
    many of them ran to a verdict (a failed test that ran counts as executed; the verdict is the
    exit code, never this label). Across the packages that listed:
      run      every selected test ran, even when the time box expired afterwards (a filter that
               selects nothing runnable in every package that carries it is vacuously run);
      partial  some selected tests ran and some did not;
      not run  none ran, or no package carrying the filter listed its tests, so nothing is claimed.
    A package that never reported (box spent, timeout, build failure) is named in
    `unlisted_packages`; its tests are unknown, so they are neither counted nor assumed."""
    carriers, bounded_carriers = {}, {}
    for package, selections in plan.items():
        for selection in selections:
            # A cap-overflow filter is never "run": it keeps only some of its matches (#2592).
            if selection.get("direct_only"):
                continue
            target = bounded_carriers if selection.get("bounded") else carriers
            target.setdefault(selection["filter"], set()).add(package)
    listed = {item["package"] for item in subsets if item.get("package")}
    seen = {}
    for item in subsets:
        for name, counts in (item.get("filters") or {}).items():
            total = seen.setdefault(name, [0, 0])
            total[0] += counts[0]
            total[1] += counts[1]
    run, partial, not_run = [], [], []
    for name in sorted(carriers):
        selected, executed = seen.get(name, (0, 0))
        if executed and executed == selected:
            run.append(name)
        elif executed:
            partial.append({"filter": name, "selected": selected, "executed": executed})
        elif not selected and carriers[name] <= listed:
            run.append(name)
        else:
            not_run.append(name)
    unlisted = sorted({package for package in plan if package not in listed})
    kept = {}
    for item in subsets:
        for name, counts in (item.get("bounded") or {}).items():
            total = kept.setdefault(name, [0, 0, 0])
            for index, count in enumerate(counts):
                total[index] += count
    bounded = [{"filter": name, "kept": kept.get(name, (0, 0, 0))[0], "executed": kept.get(name, (0, 0, 0))[1],
                "deferred": kept.get(name, (0, 0, 0))[2]} for name in sorted(bounded_carriers)]
    return {"selected": len(carriers), "run": run, "partial": partial, "not_run": not_run,
            "unlisted_packages": unlisted, "bounded": bounded,
            "deferred_tests": sum(int(item.get("deferred_tests") or 0) for item in subsets)}


def report_subset(plan, subsets, skipped_filters, dry_run):
    """Fill the targeted-subset receipt fields and say what the merge-queue gate must still cover."""
    if dry_run:
        return
    coverage = filter_coverage(plan, subsets)
    tests = {"selected_tests": sum(item.get("selected_tests", 0) for item in subsets),
             "run_tests": sum(item.get("run_tests", 0) for item in subsets)}
    changed = [item.get("changed_module_tests") or [0, 0] for item in subsets]
    direct = [item.get("direct_observer_tests") or [0, 0] for item in subsets]
    if RECEIPT.get("direct_observers"):
        tests.update(direct_observer_tests_selected=sum(c[0] for c in direct),
                     direct_observer_tests_run=sum(c[1] for c in direct))
    if RECEIPT.get("changed_modules"):
        tests.update(changed_module_tests_selected=sum(c[0] for c in changed),
                     changed_module_tests_run=sum(c[1] for c in changed))
    partial, not_run = coverage["partial"], coverage["not_run"]
    RECEIPT.update(selected_filters=coverage["selected"], run_filters=len(coverage["run"]), **tests,
                   box_expired=bool(skipped_filters) or any(item.get("box_expired") for item in subsets),
                   filters_partial=partial[:20], filters_partial_total=len(partial),
                   filters_not_run=not_run[:20], filters_not_run_total=len(not_run),
                   unlisted_packages=coverage["unlisted_packages"])
    if coverage["bounded"]:
        record_deferred(coverage)
    print(f"scoped-test: targeted subset: ran {RECEIPT['run_filters']} of {coverage['selected']} filters "
          f"completely ({len(partial)} partial, {len(not_run)} not run; "
          f"{tests['run_tests']} of {tests['selected_tests']} listed tests)"
          + (f"; unlisted packages: {', '.join(coverage['unlisted_packages'])}" if coverage["unlisted_packages"] else "")
          + "; " + ("the time box ended the run and the merge-queue gate covers the rest" if partial or not_run
                    else "the whole plan fit the time box"), flush=True)


def desktop_lane_selected(repo, receipt_data, base, head):
    """Whether the desktop lane runs: the derivation's own selection, else the gate's read of the diff.

    Raises `DESKTOP_GATE.SelectorMissing` when the candidate lacks `scripts/desktop-test-gate.py` and its diff
    touches it or one of the lane's inputs (#355)."""
    derived = receipt_data.get("desktop")
    if derived and derived.get("error"):
        raise DESKTOP_GATE.SelectorMissing(derived["error"])
    if derived:
        return derived["selected"]
    return DESKTOP_GATE.lane_selected(repo, base, head)


def announce_execution_order(python_commands, packages, dry_run, desktop_commands=()):
    """Say, and record in the receipt, which phases run in which order (#2647).

    The order is fixed: every selected Python test file runs first, fail-fast, and Cargo starts only when
    they all pass, so a red or unavailable Python runner is the whole result of the run. A dry run prints
    the same selection the real run executes and starts neither phase."""
    order = [name for name, count in (("python", len(python_commands)), ("desktop", len(desktop_commands)),
                                      ("cargo", len(packages))) if count]
    RECEIPT["phase_order"] = order
    if not order:
        return
    python = (f"Python first ({len(python_commands)} test file(s), fail-fast: the first failure or "
              "an unavailable runner stops the run before Cargo starts)" if python_commands else "")
    desktop = ("the desktop lane (npm test, then cargo test in desktop/src-tauri; only timeouts stop before Cargo)"
               if desktop_commands else "")
    cargo = (f"Cargo second ({len(packages)} package(s): {' '.join(packages)})" if packages
             else "no Cargo package scheduled")
    print("scoped-test: execution order: " + ", then ".join(part for part in (python, desktop, cargo) if part)
          + ("; this dry run executes no Python test, no desktop test and no Cargo" if dry_run else ""),
          flush=True)


def preview_python_test(command):
    """Dry-run entry of one selected Python test: the receipt entry a real run would start from.

    Same fields as a real run, planned instead of executed. Only the runner preflight runs (an in-process
    module lookup for this interpreter), never the suite itself."""
    unavailable = PYTHON_GATE.runner_status(command)
    if unavailable:
        RECEIPT["python"].append({**unavailable, "completed": False})
        print(f"scoped-test: runner_unavailable runner={unavailable['runner']} executed_tests=0 "
              f"test_file={command[-1]}: {unavailable['reason']} (a real run stops here, before Cargo)", flush=True)
        return
    RECEIPT["python"].append({"test_file": command[-1], "status": "planned", "completed": False,
                              "runner": PYTHON_GATE.command_runner(command), "executed_tests": None})


def run_python_tests(python_commands, args, log_dir, run_started, env):
    """Run the selected Python tests; the exit code on failure, else None."""
    for index, command in enumerate(python_commands):
        print("python test: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
        if args.dry_run:
            preview_python_test(command)
            continue
        # 300s, not 120s: test_scoped_test.py alone takes ~80s on a loaded host and timed out the #2416 job.
        budget = min(args.runtime_max_sec, 300)
        if args.total_max_sec is not None:
            budget = min(budget, args.total_max_sec - (time.monotonic() - run_started))
        if budget <= 0:
            print("scoped_test_timeout: total budget exhausted before Python tests", flush=True)
            return 1
        runner = PYTHON_GATE.command_runner(command)
        unavailable = PYTHON_GATE.runner_status(command)
        if unavailable:
            # A missing runner is a typed prerequisite, never a test result: zero
            # tests executed, no silent substitute runner, and the job is red.
            RECEIPT["python"].append({**unavailable, "completed": False})
            print(f"scoped-test: runner_unavailable runner={runner} executed_tests=0 "
                  f"test_file={command[-1]}: {unavailable['reason']}", flush=True)
            return 1
        with (log_dir / f"python-{index}.log").open("w") as log:
            result = {"test_file": command[-1], "status": "running", "completed": False,
                      "runner": runner, "executed_tests": None}
            RECEIPT["python"].append(result)
            def emit_python(line):
                print(line, end="", flush=True)
                log.write(line)
                log.flush()
                ran = re.match(r"Ran (\d+) tests? in ", line)
                if ran and runner == "unittest":
                    result["executed_tests"] = int(ran.group(1))
            try:
                code = stream(command, emit_python, timeout=budget, cwd=Path(args.repo).resolve(), env=env)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                result["status"] = "timed out"
                print("scoped_test_timeout: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
                return 1
        result.update(status="failed" if code else "passed", completed=True)
        if not code and runner == "unittest" and not result["executed_tests"]:
            # Exit 0 with zero executed tests (Python before 3.12 exits 0 here) is
            # no evidence: a function-style suite that merely imports unittest.
            result["status"] = "no_tests_executed"
            print("scoped-test: Python suite executed 0 tests: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
            return 1
        if code:
            print("scoped-test: Python test failed: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
            return code
    return None


def run_desktop_lane(desktop_commands, args, log_dir, run_started, env=None):
    """Run (or, on a dry run, preview) the selected desktop lane; the exit code on failure, else None.

    One receipt entry per lane run, with the same fields either way. A missing runner (node, npm,
    cargo, or on Linux pkg-config and webkit2gtk-4.1) is the typed `runner_unavailable` with zero
    executed tests, the job is red, and nothing substitutes for it (hub #3001)."""
    for index, command in enumerate(desktop_commands):
        print("desktop lane: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
        unavailable = DESKTOP_GATE.runner_status()
        if unavailable:
            RECEIPT["desktop"].append({**unavailable, "completed": False})
            print(f"scoped-test: runner_unavailable runner={unavailable['runner']} executed_tests=0 "
                  f"lane=desktop: {unavailable['reason']}" + (" (a real run records failure and continues to Cargo)"
                                                              if args.dry_run else ""), flush=True)
            if args.dry_run:
                continue
            return 1
        if args.dry_run:
            RECEIPT["desktop"].append({"lane": "desktop", "status": "planned", "completed": False,
                                       "runner": "npm+cargo", "executed_tests": None})
            continue
        budget = args.runtime_max_sec
        if args.total_max_sec is not None:
            budget = min(budget, args.total_max_sec - (time.monotonic() - run_started))
        if budget <= 0:
            print("scoped_test_timeout: total budget exhausted before the desktop lane", flush=True)
            return 1
        result = {"lane": "desktop", "status": "running", "completed": False, "runner": "npm+cargo",
                  "executed_tests": None}
        RECEIPT["desktop"].append(result)
        with (log_dir / f"desktop-{index}.log").open("w") as log:
            def emit_desktop(line):
                print(line, end="", flush=True)
                log.write(line)
                log.flush()
                if line.startswith(DESKTOP_GATE.RESULT_MARK):
                    try:
                        reported = json.loads(line[len(DESKTOP_GATE.RESULT_MARK):])
                    except ValueError:
                        return
                    result.update({key: reported[key] for key in
                                   ("npm_tests", "cargo_tests", "executed_tests", "failed_step") if key in reported})
                    result["lane_status"] = reported.get("status")
            try:
                code = stream(command, emit_desktop, timeout=budget, cwd=Path(args.repo).resolve(), env=env)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                result["status"] = "timed out"
                print("scoped_test_timeout: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
                return 1
        result.update(completed=True, status="failed" if code else "passed")
        if result.get("lane_status") in ("runner_unavailable", "no_tests_executed"):
            result["status"] = result["lane_status"]
        if not code and result.get("lane_status") != "passed":
            # Exit 0 without the lane's own passed verdict (no result line, zero tests) is no evidence.
            result["status"] = "no_tests_executed"
            code = 1
        if result.get("lane_status") == DESKTOP_GATE.TIMED_OUT:
            print("scoped_test_timeout: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
            return code or 1
        if code:
            print("scoped-test: desktop lane failed: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
            return code
    return None


def integrated_base(repo, base, head, rolling="origin/rolling"):
    """Base that excludes already-gated rolling work a launch-pin worker merged in (#2135).

    A worker branched from an unlanded pin diffs against that pin, so the rolling tip it
    was required to merge shows up as its own change. Return a synthetic commit whose tree
    is the pin merged with the rolling commit `head` integrated; the diff from it to `head`
    is the worker-owned delta. Any failure keeps `base`; landing gates still see the full candidate."""
    def git(*a, **kw):
        proc = subprocess.Popen(["git", "-C", str(repo), *a], text=True, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                stderr=subprocess.PIPE, **kw)
        out, err = proc.communicate()
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(proc.args, proc.returncode, out, err)
    try:
        if git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", rolling + "^{commit}").returncode:
            return base
        mb = git("merge-base", head, rolling)
        if mb.returncode or not mb.stdout.strip():
            return base
        integrated = mb.stdout.strip()
        base_sha = git("rev-parse", "--verify", base + "^{commit}").stdout.strip()
        if not base_sha or integrated == base_sha:
            return base
        for a, b in ((base_sha, integrated), (integrated, base_sha)):
            if git("merge-base", "--is-ancestor", a, b).returncode == 0:
                return base  # pin already landed (or rolling is behind it): nothing extra to exclude
        tree = git("merge-tree", "--write-tree", base_sha, integrated)
        if tree.returncode:
            return base
        env = dict(os.environ, GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="scoped-test", GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="scoped-test@localhost",
                   GIT_COMMITTER_NAME="scoped-test", GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL="scoped-test@localhost")
        commit = git("commit-tree", tree.stdout.split()[0], "-p", base_sha, "-p", integrated,
                     "-m", "scoped-test integrated base", env=env)
        return commit.stdout.strip() or base
    except OSError:
        return base


def main():
    with BuildAdmission() as admission:
        return run(admission)


def run(admission):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--base", default="origin/rolling")
    parser.add_argument("--head", default="HEAD")
    parser.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    parser.add_argument("--filter", "--test-filter", action="append", dest="filters", metavar="PACKAGE=FILTER",
                        help="explicit focused selection, repeatable; replaces diff-derived filters")
    parser.add_argument("--runtime-max-sec", type=positive, default=1800,
                        help="wall-clock cap per package, including build (default 1800)")
    parser.add_argument("--total-max-sec", type=positive, default=None,
                        help="wall-clock cap for the whole run; later packages get only the time left")
    parser.add_argument("--slot-wait-max-sec", type=positive, default=600,
                        help="longest to queue for a governor build slot; the wait never counts against "
                             "--runtime-max-sec or --total-max-sec (default 600)")
    parser.add_argument("--job-max-sec", type=positive, default=None,
                        help="daemon job wall budget, including waits; reserve 120s for execution")
    parser.add_argument("--filter-budget", type=int, default=FILTER_BUDGET, metavar="N",
                        help="a plan with more than N distinct filters is a targeted subset: it runs as named, "
                             "most specific first, inside --test-time-box-sec (receipt `plan: targeted_subset`); "
                             f"0 keeps the precise plan unboxed (default {FILTER_BUDGET})")
    parser.add_argument("--test-time-box-sec", type=float, default=TEST_TIME_BOX_SEC, metavar="SEC",
                        help="test time (build excluded) a targeted subset may spend before the rest is left "
                             f"to the merge-queue gate; 0 = no box (default {TEST_TIME_BOX_SEC})")
    parser.add_argument("--runner", choices=("auto", "nextest", "libtest"), default="auto",
                        help="test runner: cargo-nextest when installed (auto), else concurrent libtest "
                             "processes; RSI_SCOPED_TEST_RUNNER overrides")
    parser.add_argument("--cargo-slot", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    parser.add_argument("--cpu-quota", type=positive, default=200,
                        help="the job's Linux CPU quota in percent (default 200, two CPUs): the ceiling for "
                             "load-adaptive test threads")
    parser.add_argument("--test-shards", type=positive, default=None,
                        help="test threads per package (nextest) or concurrent single-threaded libtest "
                             "processes (default: clamp((host threads - 1-minute load) / 2, 2, 12), at most "
                             "--cpu-quota in cores)")
    parser.add_argument("--reclaim-incremental", action="store_true",
                        help="remove Cargo incremental caches after a successful package before the next package")
    parser.add_argument("--tmpfs", action="store_true",
                        help="require tmpfs parity; report unavailable and skip if no tmpfs exists "
                             "(the default uses tmpfs when available, otherwise runs normally)")
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="derive and print the plan without cargo or systemd")
    parser.add_argument("--warning-baseline-from", metavar="LOG",
                        help="print the tools/warning-baseline.json packages a finished job LOG measured, then exit")
    parser.add_argument("--execute-plan", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    parser.add_argument("--hold-admission", action="store_true", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    args = parser.parse_args()
    if args.hold_admission:
        return hold_admission()
    if args.warning_baseline_from:
        print(json.dumps(baseline_from_log(Path(args.warning_baseline_from).read_text().splitlines()),
                         indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        return 0
    if args.execute_plan:
        return execute_package(json.loads(Path(args.execute_plan).read_text()))
    RECEIPT.pop("admission", None)
    RECEIPT.update(emit=True, base=args.base, head=args.head, dry_run=args.dry_run, python=[], desktop=[],
                   tool=tool_copy())
    print("scoped-test: tool copy " + json.dumps(RECEIPT["tool"], sort_keys=True), flush=True)
    repo = Path(args.repo).resolve()
    run_started = time.monotonic()
    job_deadline = None
    if args.job_max_sec is not None:
        # The wrapper starts the wall clock before sandbox/artifact-lock waits.
        elapsed = max(0, time.time() - float(os.environ.get("RSI_JOB_STARTED_AT", time.time())))
        run_started -= elapsed
        job_deadline = run_started + args.job_max_sec - 60
    if args.filters is None and os.environ.get("RSI_SCOPED_TEST_FILTERS"):
        args.filters = [line for line in os.environ["RSI_SCOPED_TEST_FILTERS"].split("\n") if line]
    tmpfs, reason = tmpfs_parent()
    RECEIPT.update(scratch_mode="tmpfs" if tmpfs else "host", scratch_parent=tmpfs,
                   parity_unavailable=reason or None)
    if tmpfs is None:
        print(f"TMPFS_PARITY unavailable: {reason}; " +
              ("lander tmpfs parity was not checked" if args.tmpfs else
               "running selected tests with the host TMPDIR"), flush=True)
        if args.tmpfs:
            RECEIPT["scratch_mode"] = "unavailable"
            return 0
    else:
        print(f"scoped-test: selected tests use tmpfs TMPDIR under {tmpfs}", flush=True)
    checker_path = SCRIPT.with_name("check-touched-shards")
    checker = runpy.run_path(str(checker_path))
    receipt_data, derive_code = {}, 0
    bounded_plan = None
    if args.filters is not None:
        filters = args.filters
        head = checker["git_out"](repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", args.head + "^{commit}").strip()
        print(f"explicit filters: head={head} repo={repo}", flush=True)
    else:
        derive_base = integrated_base(repo, args.base, args.head)
        if derive_base != args.base:
            RECEIPT["integrated_base"] = derive_base
            print(f"scoped-test: launch pin {args.base} integrated with rolling; "
                  f"deriving the worker-owned delta from {derive_base}", flush=True)
        derive = [sys.executable, str(checker_path), "--repo", str(repo), "--base", derive_base,
                  "--head", args.head, "--filters-only", "--json"]
        print("derive: " + command_text(derive), flush=True)
        receipt = subprocess.run(derive, text=True, capture_output=True)
        if receipt.stderr:
            print(receipt.stderr, end="", file=sys.stderr)
        derive_code = receipt.returncode
        try:
            receipt_data = json.loads(receipt.stdout)
        except ValueError:
            if not derive_code:
                raise
        note_broad_observers(receipt_data)
        if derive_code:
            RECEIPT["derive_failure"] = derive_failure_reasons(receipt_data, derive_code)
            print("scoped-test: derivation failed: " + "; ".join(RECEIPT["derive_failure"]), flush=True)
            print(receipt.stdout, end="")
        else:
            filters, head = receipt_data["suggested_filters"], receipt_data["head"]
            if receipt_data.get("unfiltered_leaf_paths"):
                filters = list(dict.fromkeys([
                    *filters, *receipt_data.get("worker_filters", []),
                    *receipt_data.get("scanned_token_filters", []),
                ]))
            bounded_plan = bounded_worker_selection(receipt_data)
            if bounded_plan:
                filters = list(dict.fromkeys([*bounded_plan["filters"], *filters]))
    guards_failed = static_guards(repo, receipt_data.get("merge_base", args.base),
                                  receipt_data.get("head", args.head), receipt_data.get("audits", {}))
    if derive_code or guards_failed:
        return derive_code or 1
    if not args.dry_run and checker["git_out"](repo, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip() != head:
        raise ValueError("--head differs from this worktree's HEAD; use --dry-run to inspect another candidate")
    RECEIPT.update(head=head, filters=list(filters), candidate_repo=str(repo))
    slow_tier, slow_tests = slow_tier_plan(checker, repo, head, receipt_data, explicit=args.filters is not None)
    RECEIPT["slow_tier"] = slow_tier
    print("scoped-test: " + slow_tier_summary(slow_tier), flush=True)
    priority_filters = receipt_data.get("priority_filters", [])
    RECEIPT["priority_filters"] = priority_filters
    changed_modules = [item for item in receipt_data.get("changed_modules") or () if isinstance(item, dict)]
    changed_packages = {item["package"] for item in changed_modules if item.get("package")}
    if changed_modules:
        RECEIPT["changed_modules"] = [{key: item[key] for key in ("package", "module", "kind", "target", "path", "tests")
                                       if key in item} for item in changed_modules]
        print("scoped-test: tests of a changed module run before unrelated observers, smallest module first: "
              + " ".join(item.get("module") or f"{item['kind']}:{item['target']}" for item in changed_modules),
              flush=True)
    plan = selection_plan(filters, checker["shard_packages"](repo), priority_filters)
    direct_plan = direct_observer_plan(receipt_data, checker["shard_packages"](repo))
    if bounded_plan:
        announce_bounded_plan(bounded_plan, plan, head, receipt_data, direct_plan)
    if priority_filters:
        plan = dict(package_order(plan, changed_packages, set(direct_plan)))
        print("scoped-test: added/changed tests run first: " + " ".join(priority_filters), flush=True)
    RECEIPT["plan"] = "precise"
    RECEIPT["compile"] = planning_compile(args.filters is not None, args.dry_run)
    host_threads, load1 = host_capacity()
    adaptive = adaptive_threads(host_threads, load1)
    linux = sys.platform.startswith("linux")
    # #2524: min(job ceiling, host build budget) bounds every nested unit.
    budget = slice_budget_percent(linux)
    if budget is not None and budget < args.cpu_quota:
        print(f"scoped-test: --cpu-quota {args.cpu_quota:g}% exceeds the {build_slice()} budget "
              f"{budget:g}%; using the budget", flush=True)
        args.cpu_quota = min(args.cpu_quota, max(100.0, budget))
    quota_cores = max(1, int(args.cpu_quota // 100))
    test_shards = int(args.test_shards or min(adaptive, quota_cores))
    # The unit's quota follows the thread count, never the job's own ceiling.
    unit_quota = args.cpu_quota if args.test_shards else min(args.cpu_quota, test_shards * 100)
    runner, nextest_version = detect_runner(repo, args.runner)
    RECEIPT.update(runner=runner, nextest_version=nextest_version, test_threads=test_shards,
                   test_shards=test_shards, cpu_quota=unit_quota, job_cpu_quota=args.cpu_quota,
                   host_threads=host_threads, load1=load1, adaptive_threads=adaptive)
    over_budget = bool(args.filter_budget and plan_filter_count(plan) > args.filter_budget)
    # A broad observer fallback (#2105) keeps its module filters, which can name thousands of
    # tests: run them boxed like an over-budget plan, never refuse and never widen.
    boxed = over_budget or bool(args.filter_budget and RECEIPT.get("uncertain"))
    time_box = args.test_time_box_sec if boxed and args.test_time_box_sec > 0 else None
    if boxed:
        RECEIPT.update(plan="targeted_subset", filter_budget=args.filter_budget, test_time_box_sec=time_box,
                       selected_filters=plan_filter_count(plan), run_filters=None, gate_covers_rest=True)
        plan = dict(package_order(plan, changed_packages, set(direct_plan)))
        reason = (f"{plan_filter_count(plan)} filters exceed the budget of {args.filter_budget}" if over_budget
                  else f"{len(bounded_plan['bounded'])} cap-overflow module filters are bounded to their changed-module "
                       "tests and direct observers" if bounded_plan and not bounded_plan["broad_filters_left"]
                  else f"{RECEIPT['broad_filter_count']} broad observer filters could not be narrowed")
        print(f"scoped-test: targeted subset: {reason}; running them as named, most specific first, within "
              + (f"a {time_box:g}s test time box" if time_box else "no time box")
              + "; the merge-queue gate covers the rest", flush=True)
    python_tests = PYTHON_GATE.selected_tests(
        repo, PYTHON_GATE.changed_paths(repo, receipt_data.get("merge_base", args.base), head))
    python_commands = PYTHON_GATE.test_commands(repo, python_tests, sys.executable)
    # The derivation's own `desktop` selection when there is one (it diffs exactly as it selects every
    # other test); explicit filters derive nothing, so they read the diff the way the Python tests do.
    try:
        desktop_selected = desktop_lane_selected(repo, receipt_data, receipt_data.get("merge_base", args.base), head)
    except DESKTOP_GATE.SelectorMissing as error:
        # #355: no selector script in the candidate is a refusal, never an empty lane.
        print(f"scoped-test: desktop lane: {error}", flush=True)
        return 1
    desktop_commands = ([DESKTOP_GATE.lane_command(sys.executable, SCRIPT.with_name("desktop-test-gate.py"), repo)]
                        if desktop_selected else [])
    if not linux:
        print("scoped-test: systemd unavailable on this platform; no rsi-build.slice available, plain wall-clock timeout, no CPU cap", flush=True)
    log_dir = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="rsi-scoped-test-", dir="/tmp" if Path("/tmp").is_dir() else None))
    print(f"logs: {log_dir}", flush=True)
    RECEIPT["log_dir"] = str(log_dir)
    if not plan and not python_commands and not desktop_commands:
        print("scoped-test: no test filters derived; no tests run", flush=True)
    matched, failed, summaries, candidate_failures = set(), False, [], {}
    warning_payloads = []
    subsets, skipped_filters, box_left = [], set(), time_box
    python_scratch, python_env = None, None
    if tmpfs and python_commands and not args.dry_run:
        # The Python fixture tests honour TMPDIR, so the lander's tmpfs parity
        # covers them too; the marker names them so a log is never silent (#1799).
        python_scratch = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="rsi-scoped-test-tmpfs-python-", dir=tmpfs)
        python_env = {**os.environ, "TMPDIR": python_scratch, "TMP": python_scratch, "TEMP": python_scratch}
        print(f"TMPFS_PARITY python tests={len(python_commands)} tmpfs={python_scratch}", flush=True)
        RECEIPT["python_tmpfs"] = len(python_commands)
    announce_execution_order(python_commands, list(plan), args.dry_run, desktop_commands)
    try:
        python_code = run_python_tests(python_commands, args, log_dir, run_started, python_env)
    finally:
        if python_scratch:
            shutil.rmtree(python_scratch, ignore_errors=True)
    if python_code is not None:
        return python_code
    desktop_code = run_desktop_lane(desktop_commands, args, log_dir, run_started)
    desktop_failures = []
    if desktop_code is not None:
        failed = True
        # Desktop failures are never fed to the Rust-only baseline comparison.
        desktop_failures = [{"package": "desktop", "kind": "desktop", "target": "desktop",
                             "test": entry.get("failed_step") or entry.get("lane_status") or entry["status"]}
                            for entry in RECEIPT["desktop"] if entry["status"] != "passed"]
        RECEIPT["failures"] = desktop_failures
    if any(entry["status"] == "timed out" or entry.get("lane_status") == DESKTOP_GATE.TIMED_OUT
           for entry in RECEIPT["desktop"]):
        return desktop_code or 1
    if not args.dry_run and args.total_max_sec is not None and time.monotonic() - run_started >= args.total_max_sec:
        print("scoped_test_timeout: total budget exhausted before Cargo", flush=True)
        return 1
    packages = list(plan.items())
    # Hold one governor admission across the package units (#2216). Foreground
    # runs exclude the one queue wait; daemon jobs include it (#2025).
    slot, cargo = split_slot(checker["cargo_prefix"]())
    if args.cargo_slot and not os.environ.get("RSI_CARGO_SLOT_HELD"):
        slot = [args.cargo_slot] if Path(args.cargo_slot).name == "cargo-slot" else []
    admission_failed = False
    slot_waited = 0.0
    for package_index, (package, selections) in enumerate(packages):
        if box_left is not None and box_left <= 0 and not args.dry_run:
            # The box is spent: a later package is left to the merge-queue gate, not failed.
            print(f"scoped-test: targeted subset: test time box spent; {package} left to the merge-queue gate",
                  flush=True)
            skipped_filters.update(s["filter"] for s in selections)
            matched.update(s["filter"] for s in selections)
            continue
        package_plan = {"repo": str(repo), "package": package, "selections": selections,
                        "build": build_command(package, selections, cargo), "test_shards": test_shards,
                        "test_threads": test_shards, "runner": runner, "time_box_sec": box_left,
                        "changed_modules": [item for item in changed_modules if item.get("package") == package],
                        "direct_selections": direct_plan.get(package, [])}
        if runner == "nextest":
            package_plan["nextest"] = [*cargo, "nextest"]
        if slow_tests and package == slow_tier["package"]:
            package_plan["slow_tier_excluded"] = slow_tests
        if selects_e2e(selections):
            package_plan["e2e"] = {"prebuild": e2e_prebuild_command(cargo)}
        if tmpfs:
            package_plan["tmpfs"] = tmpfs
        plan_path = log_dir / f"{package}.json"
        plan_path.write_text(json.dumps(package_plan, indent=2) + "\n")
        budget = args.runtime_max_sec
        if args.total_max_sec is not None:
            left = args.total_max_sec - (time.monotonic() - run_started - (slot_waited if job_deadline is None else 0))
            if left < 1 and not args.dry_run:
                print(f"scoped_test_timeout: total budget exhausted before {package}: --total-max-sec "
                      f"{args.total_max_sec:g}; {package} was not run", flush=True)
                failed = True
                summaries.append((package, selections, True, 1, 0,
                                  {"phases": set(), "diagnostic": None, "terminated": False, "incomplete": True}))
                continue
            budget = min(budget, max(left, 1))
        wait_cap = args.slot_wait_max_sec
        if job_deadline is not None:
            wait_cap = max(0, job_deadline - 60 - time.monotonic())
            # A granted slot leaves only the job's remaining wall budget.
            budget = min(budget, max(1, job_deadline - time.monotonic()))
        needs_admission = bool(slot) and admission.process is None
        if needs_admission and wait_cap <= 0 and not args.dry_run:
            print(f"rsi-phase {int(time.time())} admission-timeout: no job budget left for {package}", flush=True)
            admission_failed = True
            break
        command, unit = bounded_command(package_plan, plan_path, budget, unit_quota, linux)
        run_budget = Budget(budget, queued=needs_admission, wait_cap=wait_cap,
                            grace=5 if linux else 0, deadline=job_deadline)
        print(f"{package} filters: " + " ".join(s["filter"] + (" (bounded)" if s.get("bounded") else "")
                                                  for s in selections if not s.get("direct_only")), flush=True)
        if any(s.get("direct_only") for s in selections):
            print(f"{package} direct observers selected: {sum(1 for s in selections if s.get('direct_only'))} filters "
                  "(listed in the package plan)", flush=True)
        if needs_admission:
            print(f"slot: {slot[0]} is held across packages before the budget starts; queue time " +
                  ("counts against the job wall budget" if job_deadline is not None else "is not counted"), flush=True)
        print("build once: " + command_text(package_plan["build"]), flush=True)
        print(f"tests: {runner} at {test_shards} thread(s) (host {host_threads} threads, load "
              f"{'n/a' if load1 is None else f'{load1:.1f}'}, cpu quota {unit_quota:g}%)", flush=True)
        if "e2e" in package_plan:
            print("e2e prebuild (RSI_E2E=1): " + command_text(package_plan["e2e"]["prebuild"]), flush=True)
        print("budget: " + command_text(command), flush=True)
        if args.dry_run:
            continue
        active, timed_out, terminated = {}, False, False
        subsets_before = len(subsets)
        completed, completion = 0, None
        phases, diagnostics = set(), []
        with (log_dir / f"{package}.log").open("w") as log:
            def emit(line):
                nonlocal timed_out, terminated, completed, completion
                print(line, end="", flush=True)
                log.write(line)
                log.flush()
                if line.startswith("SCOPED_START "):
                    active[line.strip().split(" ", 1)[1]] = time.monotonic()
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_END "):
                    if active.pop(line.strip().split(" ", 1)[1], None) is not None:
                        completed += 1
                        if completed % 10 == 0:
                            snapshot_package({"package": package, "status": "running",
                                              "completed": completed, "phase": "incomplete",
                                              "log": str(log_dir / f"{package}.log")})
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_PHASE "):
                    phases.add(line.strip().split(" ", 1)[1])
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_DIAG "):
                    try:
                        diagnostics.append(json.loads(line.split(" ", 1)[1]))
                    except ValueError:
                        pass
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_WARNINGS "):
                    try:
                        warning_payloads.append(json.loads(line.split(" ", 1)[1]))
                    except ValueError:
                        warning_payloads.append({"degraded": ["a SCOPED_WARNINGS line was not valid JSON"]})
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_MATCH "):
                    matched.add(line.strip().split(" ", 1)[1])
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_SUBSET "):
                    try:
                        subsets.append(json.loads(line.split(" ", 1)[1]))
                    except ValueError:
                        pass
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_IGNORED_ONLY "):
                    RECEIPT.setdefault("skipped_ignored_only", []).append(line.strip().split(" ", 1)[1])
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_SLOW_TIER_SKIPPED "):
                    try:
                        RECEIPT["slow_tier"]["skipped_tests"] += int(line.split()[1])
                    except (ValueError, IndexError):
                        pass
                elif line.startswith("SCOPED_FAILURE "):
                    target_name, test_name = line.strip().split(" ", 2)[1:]
                    kind, target_name = target_name.split(":", 1)
                    candidate_failures.setdefault(package, []).append(
                        {"kind": kind, "target": target_name, "test": test_name})
                    # Durable at once: a later package that times out, or a
                    # run that dies before the comparison, keeps what failed (#1895).
                    RECEIPT["failures"] = failure_records(candidate_failures) + desktop_failures
                else:
                    receipt = re.fullmatch(r"SCOPED_COMPLETE " + re.escape(package) + r" ([0-9]+)\n?", line)
                    if receipt:
                        completion = int(receipt[1])
                if re.search(r"(?:result[:= ]+|Result=)['\"]?timeout", line, re.I):
                    timed_out = True
                if re.search(r"code=(?:killed|dumped)\b", line):
                    terminated = True
                outcome = re.search(r"(?:Finished with result:|Result=)\s*['\"]?([\w-]+)", line)
                if outcome and outcome[1] != "success":
                    terminated = True

            def stop_unit():
                # The unique unit name belongs to this invocation only.
                subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "stop", unit], check=False, timeout=30)

            try:
                # systemd owns the primary deadline; this watchdog also bounds
                # a stuck systemd client. Other platforms use the plain timeout.
                code = admission.acquire(slot, emit, run_budget, wait_cap, job_deadline) if slot else 0
                if code == 0:
                    # Admission supplies cargo-slot's build settings and HELD marker to
                    # every unit, including baseline comparison units, without nesting slots.
                    command, unit = bounded_command(package_plan, plan_path, budget, unit_quota, linux)
                    if needs_admission:
                        emit("admitted budget: " + command_text(command) + "\n")
                    code = stream(command, emit, stop=stop_unit if linux else None, budget=run_budget)
                    if slot and admission.process.poll() is not None:
                        emit("scoped-test: shared build-slot holder exited during the package run\n")
                        code = code or 1
                timed_out |= code != 0 and run_budget.ran_out()
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                code, timed_out = 124, run_budget.started is not None
                if not timed_out:
                    emit(f"scoped_test_slot_wait_timeout: no build slot after {wait_cap:g}s; "
                         f"{package} was not run\n")
            except OSError as error:
                emit(f"scoped-test: unable to run bounded {package} command: {error}\n")
                code = 1
            if job_deadline is not None and code == 124 and run_budget.started is None:
                admission_failed = True
                emit(f"rsi-phase {int(time.time())} admission-timeout: no build slot within remaining job budget; {package} was not run\n")
            # systemd-run can exit zero after systemctl stop. Only the worker
            # can attest that every selected test finished successfully.
            if terminated or active or completion != completed:
                emit(f"scoped_test_incomplete: missing successful package completion or terminated run; "
                     f"{completed} completed tests\n")
                code = code or 1
            if timed_out:
                emit("scoped_test_timeout: slowest still-running tests: " +
                     (", ".join(f"{name} ({time.monotonic() - since:.1f}s)" for name, since in
                                sorted(active.items(), key=lambda item: item[1])) or "none (build/list/startup)") + "\n")
            package_waited = run_budget.waited if needs_admission else 0.0
            slot_waited += package_waited
            if slot:
                RECEIPT["admission"] = {"count": 1, "waited_sec": round(slot_waited, 3)}
            if box_left is not None:
                box_left -= sum(item.get("test_sec", 0) for item in subsets[subsets_before:])
            if slot:
                emit(f"scoped-test: {package}: waited {package_waited:.0f}s for a build slot "
                     f"({'counted against the job budget' if job_deadline is not None else 'not counted against the budget'})\n")
            summaries.append((package, selections, timed_out, code, completed,
                              {"phases": phases, "diagnostic": diagnostics[0] if diagnostics else None,
                               "terminated": terminated, "incomplete": bool(active) or (completion != completed and "test_failed" not in phases)}))
            failed |= timed_out or code != 0
            if not args.dry_run:
                phase = next((name for name in ("build_failed", "list_failed") if name in phases), None)
                status = "timed out" if timed_out else "failed" if code else "passed" if completed else "not applicable"
                entry = {"package": package, "status": status, "completed": completed}
                if slot:
                    entry.update(slot_wait_sec=round(package_waited, 3),
                                 run_sec=round(time.monotonic() - run_budget.started, 3)
                                 if run_budget.started is not None else 0)
                if phase:
                    entry.update(phase=phase, log=str(log_dir / f"{package}.log"))
                    if diagnostics:
                        entry["diagnostic"] = diagnostics[0]
                snapshot_package(entry)
                # Keep the last finished package durable if the outer job unit
                # is stopped before the final comparison and receipt.
            if phases.intersection({"build_failed", "list_failed"}):
                # A broken build/list makes later package results unavailable
                # to the candidate. Keep their independent landing coverage in
                # the merge queue instead of spending this job's budget there.
                break
            if admission_failed or (slot and (not admission.environment or
                                              admission.process.poll() is not None)):
                break
            if (args.reclaim_incremental and package_index + 1 < len(packages) and
                    code == 0 and not timed_out and completion == completed):
                reclaimed = reclaim_incremental(repo)
                print(f"scoped-test: reclaimed {reclaimed} bytes of Cargo incremental cache before next package",
                      flush=True)
    if boxed or bounded_plan:
        report_subset(plan, subsets, skipped_filters, args.dry_run)
    if not args.dry_run:
        # Replace the incremental snapshots with the canonical final package
        # verdicts (which can also include unmatched-filter results).
        package_timings = {entry["package"]: {key: entry[key] for key in ("slot_wait_sec", "run_sec")
                                             if key in entry} for entry in RECEIPT["packages"]}
        RECEIPT["packages"] = []
        RECEIPT["compile"]["execution"] = "executed" if summaries else "none_scheduled"
        # "Selected no tests" is a claim about test listings: make it only when
        # every package built and listed its binaries (#1913).
        unlisted = [item[0] for item in summaries if "listed" not in item[5]["phases"]]
        missing = set(filters) - matched if not unlisted else set()
        if unlisted and set(filters) - matched:
            print("scoped-test: filter matching not evaluated; no test listing from: " +
                  ", ".join(unlisted), file=sys.stderr)
        if missing:
            print("scoped-test: filters selected no tests: " + ", ".join(sorted(missing)), file=sys.stderr)
            RECEIPT["unmatched_filters"] = sorted(missing)
            failed = True
        for package, selections, timed_out, code, completed, detail in summaries:
            # Filter coverage is independent of a package's successful execution. A stale
            # empty observer must not relabel 90 passing tests as no_tests_selected (#397).
            unmatched = completed == 0 and any(s["filter"] in missing for s in selections)
            status = ("timed out" if timed_out else "failed" if code or unmatched else
                      "passed" if completed else "not applicable")
            phase = next((name for name in ("build_failed", "list_failed") if name in detail["phases"]), None)
            if phase is None and (timed_out or detail["terminated"] or detail["incomplete"]):
                phase = "incomplete"
            elif phase is None and "test_failed" in detail["phases"]:
                phase = "test_failed"
            elif phase is None and unmatched:
                phase = "no_tests_selected"
            elif phase is None and code:
                phase = "incomplete"
            summary = f"scoped-test: {package}: {status} ({completed} completed tests)\n"
            entry = {"package": package, "status": status, "completed": completed}
            RECEIPT["compile"]["packages"][package] = package_compile_status(detail["phases"])
            if slot:
                entry.update(package_timings.get(package, {}))
            if phase:
                entry.update(phase=phase, log=str(log_dir / f"{package}.log"))
                if detail["diagnostic"]:
                    entry["diagnostic"] = detail["diagnostic"]
                summary += (f"scoped-test: {package}: {phase}" +
                            (f": {detail['diagnostic']}" if detail["diagnostic"] else "") +
                            f" (log {entry['log']})\n")
            RECEIPT["packages"].append(entry)
            print(summary, end="", flush=True)
            with (log_dir / f"{package}.log").open("a") as log:
                log.write(summary)
    if not args.dry_run and not any(detail["phases"].intersection({"build_failed", "list_failed"})
                                    for _, _, _, _, _, detail in summaries):
        # Only a build that finished can say which warnings the candidate carries (#1897).
        gate = warning_gate(repo, receipt_data.get("merge_base", args.base), warning_payloads)
        if gate is not None:
            RECEIPT["warnings"] = gate
            failed |= report_warning_gate(gate)
    if not args.dry_run:
        base = args.base
        base_failures = {package: names for package, names in candidate_failures.items() if names}
        remaining = (args.runtime_max_sec if args.total_max_sec is None else
                     max(0.0, args.total_max_sec - (time.monotonic() - run_started)))
        admission_lost = bool(slot and admission.process is not None and admission.process.poll() is not None)
        stopped_on_build_or_list = any(
            detail["phases"].intersection({"build_failed", "list_failed"})
            for _, _, _, _, _, detail in summaries
        )
        comparison = ({"status": "not_needed", "base": base,
                       "detail": "skipped after lost admission" if admission_lost else "skipped after build/list failure"}
                      if stopped_on_build_or_list or admission_lost else
                      compare_failures_with_base(repo, base, base_failures, log_dir,
                                                 remaining, args.cpu_quota, linux, cargo, tmpfs))
        RECEIPT["failures"] = failure_records(candidate_failures) + desktop_failures
        RECEIPT["baseline_comparison"] = comparison
        RECEIPT["baseline_reds"] = comparison.get("baseline_reds", [])
        RECEIPT.update(annotate_baseline_reds(repo, RECEIPT["baseline_reds"]))
        remaining = (args.runtime_max_sec if args.total_max_sec is None else
                     max(0.0, args.total_max_sec - (time.monotonic() - run_started)))
        stale_reds, stale_detail = split_stale_base_failures(
            repo, head, base, base_failures, comparison, log_dir, remaining, args.cpu_quota, linux,
            cargo, tmpfs)
        if stale_detail:
            comparison["fork_point_comparison"] = stale_detail
        if stale_reds:
            RECEIPT["stale_base_reds"] = stale_reds
            RECEIPT["stale_base"] = (f"{len(stale_reds)} failing test(s) were already red at your branch "
                                     f"point and pass on {base}: merge {base} and re-run")
            print("scoped-test: stale_base: " + RECEIPT["stale_base"], flush=True)
        baseline_keys = {(item.get("package"), item.get("kind"), item.get("target"), item.get("test"))
                         for item in comparison.get("baseline_reds", []) + stale_reds}
        RECEIPT["new_failures"] = ([item for item in RECEIPT["failures"]
                                    if (item.get("package"), item.get("kind"), item.get("target"),
                                        item.get("test")) not in baseline_keys]
                                   if comparison.get("status") == "complete" else desktop_failures or None)
    return 124 if admission_failed else 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        code = main()
    except (OSError, ValueError) as error:
        print(f"scoped-test: {error}", file=sys.stderr)
        code = 1
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        code = 130
    record_merge_onto_rolling()
    emit_receipt(code)
    sys.exit(code)
